WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.083 --> 00:00:01.584
[Ray] About a year's profit.

00:00:01.793 --> 00:00:03.545
[Ami] Around 700,000.

00:00:03.628 --> 00:00:08.675
[Ami] I didn't even realize I had started crying. I was on the verge of a mental breakdown.

00:00:08.675 --> 00:00:11.219
[Ami] When it came to getting that bonus, it felt like I was trading my life for money.

00:00:11.219 --> 00:00:16.307
[Ami] I helped the company promote its "purpose" every day, but what was my own purpose?

00:00:16.307 --> 00:00:17.976
[Ami] I couldn't find the meaning in this job anymore.

00:00:17.976 --> 00:00:19.936
[Ami] Of course, I think work itself is meaningless.

00:00:19.936 --> 00:00:24.190
[Ami] Both of my side hustles came from something I genuinely love.

00:00:24.190 --> 00:00:27.986
[Ami] Why don't I fill a market gap like this?

00:00:27.986 --> 00:00:33.199
[Ami] And I wanted to tell the whole world that I had made this thing. When I made it, I was like, wow,

00:00:33.241 --> 00:00:33.575
[Ami] it was amazing.

00:00:33.658 --> 00:00:34.784
[Ray] Amazing.

00:00:34.826 --> 00:00:38.288
[Ami] Out of a thousand, I might have only sold a dozen or so. I thought, forget it, I should just stick to my day job.

00:00:38.288 --> 00:00:43.752
[Ami] I felt I did something I considered very great: I posted updates for 365 consecutive days.

00:00:43.835 --> 00:00:44.836
[Ray] What did you update?

00:00:44.878 --> 00:00:45.795
[Ami] My journal.

00:00:45.879 --> 00:00:50.425
[Ami] I should be more selfish. Earning this money makes me feel very valued.

00:00:50.550 --> 00:00:51.885
[Ray] Our guest for this interview is Ami.

00:00:51.885 --> 00:00:57.182
[Ray] A year and a half ago, she joined the Yikouxinfan community as a viewer of the channel, and we had the chance to meet once offline.

00:00:57.182 --> 00:01:01.936
[Ray] Back then, she had just quit her job without another one lined up, and was starting to develop her cultural and creative business and video creation full-time.

00:01:01.936 --> 00:01:06.900
[Ray] Now, over a year later, her progress has been astonishingly fast. Her cultural and creative business is doing great,

00:01:06.900 --> 00:01:10.862
[Ray] and her self-media has taken off. Her Xiaohongshu followers grew to over a hundred thousand,

00:01:10.862 --> 00:01:13.031
[Ray] and she has over 60,000 subscribers on YouTube.

00:01:13.031 --> 00:01:16.159
[Ray] From an office worker who was once pushed to the brink by her job,

00:01:16.159 --> 00:01:20.413
[Ray] she has transformed into a successful creator and entrepreneur through her passion for her side hustle.

00:01:20.413 --> 00:01:24.584
[Ray] I think Ami's experience, for many young people who are struggling and looking for a way out,

00:01:24.584 --> 00:01:26.252
[Ray] serves as an excellent reference.

00:01:26.294 --> 00:01:31.758
[Ray] In April 2025, at the Yikouxinfan Shanghai meetup, we met again, and I recorded the following conversation with her,

00:01:31.758 --> 00:01:33.593
[Ray] reviewing how she did it.

00:01:33.593 --> 00:01:37.889
[Ray] There are no ads in the middle of this podcast. If you'd like to support me, please check the sponsorship link in the comment section,

00:01:37.889 --> 00:01:43.353
[Ray] or join the Yikouxinfan community to read and meet with peers like Ami from around the world,

00:01:43.353 --> 00:01:44.312
[Ray] and make connections.

00:01:44.354 --> 00:01:46.064
[Ray] Alright, let's begin.

00:01:46.147 --> 00:01:47.857
[Ray] This is the second time we've met.

00:01:47.857 --> 00:01:50.735
[Ray] The first time was about a year and a half ago.

00:01:50.902 --> 00:01:51.319
[Ami] Right.

00:01:51.361 --> 00:01:53.154
[Ray] How long had it been since you quit your job back then?

00:01:53.238 --> 00:01:55.824
[Ami] I think it had been a little less than half a year.

00:01:55.949 --> 00:01:59.869
[Ray] We connected pretty quickly in the community, since you're in the Yikouxinfan community,

00:01:59.869 --> 00:02:02.705
[Ray] we recorded a podcast within the community.

00:02:02.705 --> 00:02:03.957
[Ray] At that time, you shared that,

00:02:03.957 --> 00:02:09.420
[Ray] your annual profit was around 500,000 RMB,

00:02:09.420 --> 00:02:13.133
[Ray] and now a year has passed. How has that number changed?

00:02:13.216 --> 00:02:18.346
[Ami] If I calculate the total, it should be around 700,000.

00:02:18.513 --> 00:02:23.601
[Ray] Mmm, because you're in the community and we've collaborated, I've actually been following your progress.

00:02:23.601 --> 00:02:28.773
[Ray] And I noticed that very quickly, your social media, specifically Xiaohongshu in China, grew to,

00:02:28.773 --> 00:02:29.732
[Ray] how many followers do you have now?

00:02:29.774 --> 00:02:32.443
[Ami] About 130,000 now. It's not that many.

00:02:32.485 --> 00:02:35.613
[Ray] 130,000 is a lot. How long did it take to get to 130,000?

00:02:35.697 --> 00:02:39.033
[Ami] I think it took about a year.

00:02:39.075 --> 00:02:40.743
[Ray] OK, so it took off.

00:02:40.743 --> 00:02:44.414
[Ray] And I've noticed you've changed quite a bit this past year.

00:02:44.414 --> 00:02:49.669
[Ray] And since you're in Shanghai for our gathering, I wanted to take this opportunity to chat with you,

00:02:49.669 --> 00:02:52.213
[Ray] to talk about your growth and changes during this time.

00:02:52.213 --> 00:02:55.842
[Ray] I believe it will be very different from our first conversation.

00:02:55.884 --> 00:02:59.345
[Ray] And I think this interview,

00:02:59.345 --> 00:03:04.684
[Ray] can also offer some inspiration to peers who are at a similar stage as you,

00:03:04.684 --> 00:03:08.521
[Ray] or were at your previous stage.

00:03:13.651 --> 00:03:14.527
[Ray] What did you do?

00:03:14.569 --> 00:03:18.781
[Ami] Before I quit my job, I actually had a few different work experiences.

00:03:18.781 --> 00:03:24.204
[Ami] Because if a corporate HR person were to interview me, they would think I'm very...

00:03:24.204 --> 00:03:28.333
[Ami] jumpy. For example, in three years of working, I changed jobs three times.

00:03:28.333 --> 00:03:33.338
[Ami] I started out in marketing, which was my first job after graduation.

00:03:33.338 --> 00:03:39.344
[Ami] At that time, it was hard to find a job, so my first company was a startup.

00:03:39.344 --> 00:03:41.054
[Ami] And to be honest,

00:03:41.054 --> 00:03:45.808
[Ami] these three work experiences all gave me some major life insights.

00:03:45.808 --> 00:03:49.062
[Ami] I may not have known it then, but looking back now, that first company,

00:03:49.062 --> 00:03:51.981
[Ami] actually helped me a lot with my later self-media work.

00:03:51.981 --> 00:03:56.319
[Ami] I think it's mainly because the boss of that startup left a deep impression on me.

00:03:56.319 --> 00:04:01.824
[Ami] It's quite interesting. Let me briefly introduce her. She's Russian, but she speaks very fluent Chinese.

00:04:01.950 --> 00:04:02.951
[Ray] A man or a woman?

00:04:02.992 --> 00:04:04.994
[Ami] A woman, a Russian female boss.

00:04:04.994 --> 00:04:09.749
[Ami] At that time, the entire company was actually built around her personal brand.

00:04:09.749 --> 00:04:15.421
[Ami] It was about helping some overseas companies enter China, and she played the role of,

00:04:15.421 --> 00:04:19.801
[Ami] a "China Marketing Expert." That was the persona she created for herself.

00:04:19.801 --> 00:04:24.973
[Ami] And through this persona, she would either get business or provide training for companies,

00:04:24.973 --> 00:04:27.266
[Ami] in short, everything revolved around her.

00:04:27.266 --> 00:04:32.981
[Ami] So, a big thing I learned from her back then was how to build a personal brand.

00:04:32.981 --> 00:04:36.484
[Ami] One thing that really struck me, something I realized on my own,

00:04:36.484 --> 00:04:39.612
[Ami] is that we often talk about the "bucket effect,"

00:04:39.612 --> 00:04:44.909
[Ami] where each person has different planks, but we're initially taught that,

00:04:44.909 --> 00:04:49.789
[Ami] you need to make every plank equally long so you can hold more water.

00:04:49.789 --> 00:04:55.003
[Ami] But what I actually learned from her was that you should develop your strengths, because she,

00:04:55.003 --> 00:04:59.799
[Ami] I was curious why she learned Chinese, so I would sometimes chat with her and ask,

00:04:59.799 --> 00:05:05.430
[Ami] "Your spoken Chinese is so good," but she actually wasn't great at writing—we used to ghostwrite for her.

00:05:05.430 --> 00:05:10.184
[Ami] I asked, "Why did you only develop your speaking skill?"

00:05:10.268 --> 00:05:13.479
[Ami] And she said it was because she felt she didn't need to be a good writer.

00:05:13.479 --> 00:05:18.484
[Ami] She didn't need to read any profound academic papers or anything like that.

00:05:18.484 --> 00:05:24.032
[Ami] She felt that as a "China Marketing Expert," what she needed most was communication,

00:05:24.032 --> 00:05:27.243
[Ami] so she just honed her speaking skill to the highest level,

00:05:27.243 --> 00:05:31.039
[Ami] but she felt that other things like reading and writing could all be outsourced.

00:05:31.080 --> 00:05:33.583
[Ami] So for her, her strength,

00:05:33.583 --> 00:05:38.212
[Ami] in her eyes, was her persona and her communication skills.

00:05:38.212 --> 00:05:40.882
[Ami] That actually had a big impact on me,

00:05:40.882 --> 00:05:44.594
[Ami] because I was always taught that you need to be well-rounded.

00:05:44.677 --> 00:05:46.304
[Ray] Morals, intellect, physical fitness, aesthetics, and labor. Right.

00:05:46.429 --> 00:05:47.722
[Ami] Then later I realized, hey,

00:05:47.722 --> 00:05:52.518
[Ami] actually, if you want to... to stand out from the crowd,

00:05:52.518 --> 00:05:55.021
[Ami] you don't need to be a jack-of-all-trades.

00:05:55.021 --> 00:05:58.941
[Ami] Instead, you should develop your unique characteristics even further.

00:05:58.941 --> 00:06:01.402
[Ami] Because you can't possibly score 100 in every single area.

00:06:01.402 --> 00:06:06.949
[Ami] But you can turn your strongest skill into your max-level skill, like in a video game.

00:06:06.949 --> 00:06:11.913
[Ami] So later I thought, okay, as for me, I think, in my self-perception,

00:06:11.913 --> 00:06:14.332
[Ami] I might have a bit of an aesthetic sense.

00:06:14.374 --> 00:06:14.874
[Ami] (laughs)

00:06:14.916 --> 00:06:20.129
[Ami] And maybe the things I photograph are quite good, and I feel pretty good about the designs I make.

00:06:20.171 --> 00:06:20.671
[Ami] (laughs)

00:06:20.671 --> 00:06:21.172
[Ami]

00:06:21.214 --> 00:06:21.839
[Ray] What was your major in university?

00:06:21.923 --> 00:06:24.258
[Ami] I studied journalism in university.

00:06:24.258 --> 00:06:27.929
[Ami] I chose that major because I liked photography,

00:06:27.929 --> 00:06:30.389
[Ami] but I later found out it was all about writing.

00:06:30.389 --> 00:07:01.129
[Ami] (laughs) So it went against what I had hoped for. Uh, but to get back to the point, I think the first thing I learned from her was that you have to develop your strengths. If you find what makes you shine, you have to make it shine even brighter. That's a way to become more dazzling among your peers, or in society as a whole. So, you mentioned earlier that you have an aesthetic sense. How did that manifest? Did it show in your first job? I think it was very simple: the boss really liked the PowerPoints I made (laughs). (laughs)

00:07:01.212 --> 00:07:04.382
[Ami] It's just that, because I... I think when you're a beast of burden at work,

00:07:04.382 --> 00:07:09.095
[Ami] your PowerPoint skill is something that can really show,

00:07:09.095 --> 00:07:12.098
[Ami] it's a key factor in whether your boss likes you or not.

00:07:12.098 --> 00:07:16.352
[Ami] It was funny back then; they called me the "graphic designer," you know?

00:07:16.352 --> 00:07:20.690
[Ami] I was originally hired to do content. In that first job, I was mainly supposed to,

00:07:20.690 --> 00:07:22.650
[Ami] for example, when a foreign brand came to China,

00:07:22.650 --> 00:07:26.821
[Ami] I was supposed to give them a strategy, on which platforms they should grow,

00:07:26.821 --> 00:07:30.366
[Ami] what kind of content they should create, and how to place ads.

00:07:30.366 --> 00:07:35.538
[Ami] But later it became, okay, after I finished the content—and others did the same kind of content—

00:07:35.538 --> 00:07:40.459
[Ami] the boss would have them give me their final products, and then I would,

00:07:40.459 --> 00:07:42.712
[Ami] do the beautification work.

00:07:42.712 --> 00:07:48.759
[Ami] That's when I thought, okay, maybe I do have a bit of a talent

00:07:48.759 --> 00:07:54.098
[Ami] for this PowerPoint skill. But later I realized, okay, it's not just about making PowerPoints,

00:07:54.098 --> 00:07:59.604
[Ami] it might be that my aesthetic sense is a little better than others'. I would think, okay, this should be placed like this,

00:07:59.604 --> 00:08:04.442
[Ami] with no particular reason, but the result would be more pleasing to the eye.

00:08:04.483 --> 00:08:08.696
[Ami] And I think that's why the boss liked to give me the final fine-tuning

00:08:08.738 --> 00:08:11.324
[Ami] work to do.

00:08:11.407 --> 00:08:15.620
[Ray] You mentioned PowerPoint. You really shouldn't underestimate the ability to make a good PowerPoint,

00:08:15.620 --> 00:08:19.499
[Ray] especially since many people say we have AI now, so nobody needs to make them anymore.

00:08:19.499 --> 00:08:25.379
[Ray] But I still think you should strive to do a good job on things that can be presented visually.

00:08:25.379 --> 00:08:29.383
[Ray] Because I still believe it's a very clever way—and "clever" in quotation marks—

00:08:29.383 --> 00:08:30.426
[Ray] a thing that can give you an advantage.

00:08:30.509 --> 00:08:34.222
[Ray] So, about that startup, why did you end up leaving?

00:08:34.222 --> 00:08:35.973
[Ray] You said you were always very... jumpy.

00:08:35.973 --> 00:08:41.812
[Ami] Mmm, how should I put it... Everyone wants to move on to better things, and I'm no exception.

00:08:41.812 --> 00:08:47.109
[Ami] At the time, I felt that, uh, this company, this startup, was probably very small in scale.

00:08:47.109 --> 00:08:52.573
[Ami] I remember there were only, uh, about seven or eight people. It was a very, very small company.

00:08:52.573 --> 00:08:58.538
[Ami] It allows you to become well-versed in many things, you get exposure to all sorts of tasks, but,

00:08:58.538 --> 00:09:04.377
[Ami] you could feel that it wasn't very structured. To put it simply, its benefits system wasn't very good.

00:09:04.377 --> 00:09:08.798
[Ami] And including its whole company structure, you'd feel like, hmm,

00:09:08.798 --> 00:09:12.426
[Ami] I'd probably hit a ceiling if I stayed for another two or three years.

00:09:12.426 --> 00:09:16.722
[Ami] Or rather, I felt that if I left after another two or three years, I might not be very competitive.

00:09:16.722 --> 00:09:20.935
[Ami] If I wanted to jump to a big company, why not do it sooner?

00:09:20.935 --> 00:09:25.690
[Ami] That was what I wanted in the first place, for instance, when I graduated. I wanted to join a big company, I just didn't have the chance.

00:09:25.690 --> 00:09:30.444
[Ami] I was just using it as a stepping stone, so I didn't want to stay there for too long.

00:09:30.444 --> 00:09:34.490
[Ami] So at that time, I kept thinking, "I want to go to a big company, I want to go to a big company."

00:09:34.490 --> 00:09:38.953
[Ami] I think maybe the heavens sensed it; you know, the law of attraction.

00:09:38.995 --> 00:09:43.791
[Ami] And coincidentally, as I said in our first podcast, this opportunity came from,

00:09:43.791 --> 00:09:49.880
[Ami] a recommendation from a colleague at my first company. It's very interesting, because at the time,

00:09:49.880 --> 00:09:53.175
[Ami] when I joined, I might not have fully understood,

00:09:53.175 --> 00:09:57.305
[Ami] but after I interviewed and got the offer, I realized, okay,

00:09:57.305 --> 00:09:59.390
[Ami] it turned out to be a very, very big company.

00:09:59.390 --> 00:10:04.895
[Ami] Uh, how do I say this... In China, it's the kind of company you hear about and you know, okay, it's a top dog.

00:10:04.937 --> 00:10:06.647
[Ray] What kind of company was it?

00:10:06.731 --> 00:10:08.441
[Ami] Uh, it was in the finance and banking industry.

00:10:08.607 --> 00:10:14.614
[Ami] And that was how, I'm not sure why, I got the opportunity to enter the finance world.

00:10:14.614 --> 00:10:18.993
[Ami] And I felt, wow, that was quite a big leap,

00:10:18.993 --> 00:10:23.831
[Ami] and I thought it would be a great help for my future career development, at least from my perspective back then.

00:10:23.831 --> 00:10:26.292
[Ami] But that company... it was kind of, uh,

00:10:26.292 --> 00:10:31.839
[Ami] it was like I, a little white rabbit, suddenly jumped into a den of big grey wolves.

00:10:31.839 --> 00:10:32.757
[Ray] So what did you do there?

00:10:32.798 --> 00:10:36.844
[Ray] You studied journalism, and you were doing graphic design before.

00:10:36.844 --> 00:10:38.888
[Ray] What did you do in finance?

00:10:38.888 --> 00:10:43.392
[Ami] Uh, I did a more traditional back-office role called PR, public relations.

00:10:43.392 --> 00:10:47.855
[Ami] It mainly involved writing press releases for the company.

00:10:47.855 --> 00:10:52.401
[Ami] It was actually a perfect match for my major. And my, uh, my boss probably valued me more because,

00:10:52.401 --> 00:10:55.905
[Ami] I had a relatively good command of English,

00:10:55.905 --> 00:10:59.033
[Ami] so I could conduct interviews for English press releases,

00:10:59.033 --> 00:11:04.538
[Ami] and help write speeches for the management. That was the content of my work.

00:11:04.538 --> 00:11:07.625
[Ami] Overall, it was very administrative, very much paperwork.

00:11:07.625 --> 00:11:11.003
[Ami] Of course, I felt these two jobs were very, very different.

00:11:11.003 --> 00:11:13.964
[Ami] Because the previous one was more creative,

00:11:13.964 --> 00:11:18.886
[Ami] you had to come up with very creative ideas to help clients promote their products.

00:11:18.886 --> 00:11:22.848
[Ami] But when you join what we call a Chinese investment bank,

00:11:22.848 --> 00:11:26.227
[Ami] it's a more serious kind of company.

00:11:26.227 --> 00:11:32.316
[Ami] And the articles you have to write are all very politically correct and proper. That's the style.

00:11:32.316 --> 00:11:35.444
[Ami] So for me, the transition felt quite significant.

00:11:35.528 --> 00:11:35.903
[Ray] Mmm.

00:11:35.945 --> 00:11:39.365
[Ami] Yes. But at that company, I think I only lasted a year.

00:11:39.365 --> 00:11:43.994
[Ami] It felt like my life force was only enough to sustain me for that one year.

00:11:44.078 --> 00:11:48.082
[Ray] Listening to you, it sounds like you... you said they were all big grey wolves. How so?

00:11:48.124 --> 00:11:49.667
[Ami] Mmm, how should I put it...

00:11:49.667 --> 00:11:55.256
[Ami] I was probably the youngest person in that department,

00:11:55.256 --> 00:11:58.342
[Ami] and perhaps the most inexperienced.

00:11:58.342 --> 00:12:02.888
[Ami] And the seniors above me were maybe ten years older. With that age gap,

00:12:02.888 --> 00:12:06.016
[Ami] you can imagine they had experienced much more than I had.

00:12:06.016 --> 00:12:11.355
[Ami] And I felt that what I learned at the startup couldn't be applied to this company.

00:12:11.355 --> 00:12:15.317
[Ami] So that one year at the company was, I think, quite painful for me,

00:12:15.317 --> 00:12:20.906
[Ami] but also a year of great growth. How should I put it... After you join this company,

00:12:20.906 --> 00:12:24.994
[Ami] everyone... they... look... I can't say they looked down on you,

00:12:24.994 --> 00:12:27.121
[Ami] but everyone saw you as just a little kid.

00:12:27.288 --> 00:12:27.955
[Ray] So they looked down on you.

00:12:27.997 --> 00:12:33.753
[Ami] Right, right, right. It was like, okay, whenever something went wrong, you were the first one to take the blame.

00:12:33.753 --> 00:12:38.424
[Ami] And when there were no problems, all the work was piled on you first. That was my role.

00:12:38.424 --> 00:12:42.845
[Ami] But I can only say that this one year, in my life,

00:12:42.845 --> 00:12:46.307
[Ami] was a year of very, very rapid growth.

00:12:46.307 --> 00:12:50.811
[Ami] But I also feel there was... how to say... a certain interesting aspect to joining that company.

00:12:50.811 --> 00:12:56.609
[Ami] I probably thought that after joining a big company, I would be like all those...

00:12:56.609 --> 00:12:59.445
[Ami] There was a huge contrast. When I was at the startup,

00:12:59.445 --> 00:13:02.448
[Ami] there was no dress code. You could dress very casually.

00:13:02.448 --> 00:13:06.202
[Ami] Then every time I passed through a station in Hong Kong called Central Station,

00:13:06.202 --> 00:13:10.664
[Ami] which is where all the financial firms are, I would think, "Wow, everyone's so fancy."

00:13:10.664 --> 00:13:15.920
[Ami] They dressed very business-like, and everyone seemed to walk with such purpose.

00:13:15.920 --> 00:13:21.425
[Ami] But when I actually joined the kind of company I'd been aspiring to,

00:13:21.425 --> 00:13:23.761
[Ami] I found that, okay, I was also dressed to the nines,

00:13:23.761 --> 00:13:27.515
[Ami] but the work I was doing still made me feel like a cog in the machine.

00:13:27.556 --> 00:13:33.395
[Ami] So that was a pretty big shock to me. The glamorous side that I saw...

00:13:33.395 --> 00:13:37.858
[Ami] was it really that glamorous once I actually joined the company?

00:13:37.858 --> 00:13:40.945
[Ami] So that's also why I started to think,

00:13:40.945 --> 00:13:44.281
[Ami] "Maybe I don't want to keep doing this." It was the beginning of that thought.

00:13:44.281 --> 00:13:49.453
[Ami] And in the end, I think it was partly because I had this thought,

00:13:49.453 --> 00:13:55.376
[Ami] and partly because my side hustle was starting to show some growth. So I quit that job.

00:13:55.417 --> 00:13:58.671
[Ray] Okay, let's not talk about your side hustle just yet.

00:13:58.671 --> 00:14:03.717
[Ray] You just said you couldn't stand it in that place full of big bad wolves.

00:14:03.717 --> 00:14:05.469
[Ray] Can you give a specific example?

00:14:05.469 --> 00:14:09.557
[Ray] What was it that pushed you to the point where you felt like you didn't want to do it anymore?

00:14:09.557 --> 00:14:12.810
[Ray] I seem to recall you mentioned this in your video about quitting without a new job lined up.

00:14:13.018 --> 00:14:16.105
[Ami] Yes, I think it was an accumulation of things.

00:14:16.105 --> 00:14:21.986
[Ami] It wasn't like a single incident that made me make such an impulsive decision.

00:14:21.986 --> 00:14:26.574
[Ami] I was in a very high-pressure environment. I remember it clearly.

00:14:26.574 --> 00:14:32.162
[Ami] There was this task assigned to me, and it wasn't actually part of my job description.

00:14:32.162 --> 00:14:37.710
[Ami] It was outside the scope of my duties. But another manager needed it urgently,

00:14:37.710 --> 00:14:42.965
[Ami] and I was the most junior person in the whole department, so whenever there was an ad-hoc task like this,

00:14:42.965 --> 00:14:44.508
[Ami] it would be handed to me.

00:14:44.508 --> 00:14:45.259
[Ami] But I...

00:14:45.342 --> 00:14:51.473
[Ami] I was expecting to get some guidance, for someone to tell me how I should go about

00:14:51.473 --> 00:14:52.016
[Ami] doing it.

00:14:52.057 --> 00:14:57.771
[Ami] But I later realized that, okay, in an environment like that, you have to figure things out on your own.

00:14:57.771 --> 00:15:03.193
[Ami] No one will tell you what to do. You just have to bite the bullet and do it, and then see what happens.

00:15:03.193 --> 00:15:08.407
[Ami] But at that time, I felt completely clueless, and then various other things kept piling on.

00:15:08.407 --> 00:15:14.288
[Ami] And I remember that day very clearly. I worked overtime until about 11 PM, and the office was empty.

00:15:14.288 --> 00:15:17.499
[Ami] There was just a light on above my little cubicle.

00:15:17.499 --> 00:15:22.922
[Ami] It was like a spotlight on a stage, but I was this very miserable figure.

00:15:22.963 --> 00:15:28.510
[Ami] And I was on the verge of a breakdown because my boss needed this task the next day,

00:15:28.510 --> 00:15:31.805
[Ami] but I had absolutely no idea how to do it.

00:15:31.805 --> 00:15:37.478
[Ami] So as I was typing, I didn't even realize I had started crying until...

00:15:37.478 --> 00:15:43.025
[Ami] I felt tears dripping onto my hands. Okay, I realized I had reached a state of mental breakdown.

00:15:43.025 --> 00:15:47.821
[Ami] But even in that situation, I knew, okay, the boss needs this tomorrow, so I have to get it done.

00:15:47.821 --> 00:15:52.034
[Ami] But that incident made me suddenly realize that I couldn't go on like this anymore.

00:15:52.034 --> 00:15:57.164
[Ami] I had reached a breaking point, mentally. If I continued like this,

00:15:57.164 --> 00:15:58.207
[Ami] I might not be able to hold on.

00:15:58.207 --> 00:16:02.628
[Ami] So it was an event like this that made me suddenly start thinking, okay, if I just hold on for a few more months,

00:16:02.628 --> 00:16:04.588
[Ami] I could get my bonus.

00:16:04.588 --> 00:16:08.592
[Ami] But at that moment, I thought, okay, I can't take this bonus. If I go for this bonus,

00:16:08.634 --> 00:16:11.804
[Ami] it feels like I'd be trading my life for money.

00:16:11.804 --> 00:16:15.140
[Ami] So that incident had a huge impact on me.

00:16:15.140 --> 00:16:20.312
[Ami] Then later, after I got home, maybe a few days passed, I saw various videos of yours,

00:16:20.312 --> 00:16:26.402
[Ami] and I thought, okay, maybe it's time for me to think about changing my way of life.

00:16:26.402 --> 00:16:30.572
[Ray] Mmm, right. Anyway, I'm not responsible for anyone who quits their job after watching my videos.

00:16:30.572 --> 00:16:36.578
[Ray] Right, it's basically the same in our community. Many people say, "I watched your 'lying flat' video,"

00:16:36.578 --> 00:16:39.164
[Ray] "and watched your other stuff," and later when we did that...

00:16:39.164 --> 00:16:40.833
[Ray] ...that annual VIP video. "I watched it and then I quit."

00:16:40.833 --> 00:16:44.670
[Ray] I'm like, okay, I'm not responsible. It's everyone's own responsibility.

00:16:44.670 --> 00:16:48.549
[Ray] So you said your job was so busy, how did you have time for a side hustle?

00:16:48.549 --> 00:16:49.800
[Ray] What was this side hustle?

00:16:49.925 --> 00:16:53.887
[Ami] I was actually doing my own media content creation at the same time.

00:16:53.887 --> 00:16:57.599
[Ami] I actually started doing it before I entered the workforce.

00:16:57.599 --> 00:17:02.438
[Ami] But for example, when work was stressful, I might post a little less.

00:17:02.438 --> 00:17:05.649
[Ami] And when work was a bit less stressful, I'd post a bit more.

00:17:05.733 --> 00:17:06.734
[Ray] On which platform?

00:17:06.817 --> 00:17:11.739
[Ami] It was on Bilibili. Back then, Bilibili was still growing rapidly, and...

00:17:11.780 --> 00:17:12.489
[Ray] It's still booming now.

00:17:12.531 --> 00:17:14.700
[Ami] Right, it's still booming now.

00:17:14.700 --> 00:17:18.787
[Ami] And at that time, I would also receive a lot of comments from viewers.

00:17:18.787 --> 00:17:24.251
[Ami] For me, it was like having yin on one side and yang on the other. Having that balance

00:17:24.251 --> 00:17:29.089
[Ami] made things a bit better. When you put your energy out there,

00:17:29.089 --> 00:17:34.344
[Ami] you also get some good feedback. I felt that was a measure that could balance out the stress from work,

00:17:34.344 --> 00:17:37.264
[Ami] or the negative energy.

00:17:37.264 --> 00:17:42.519
[Ami] And back then, my content was mainly about lifestyle.

00:17:42.519 --> 00:17:45.689
[Ami] It also included my journaling, because that was...

00:17:45.689 --> 00:17:49.818
[Ami] Journaling content was what got my channel started.

00:17:49.818 --> 00:17:53.030
[Ami] So I've always persisted in making that kind of content.

00:17:53.030 --> 00:17:56.784
[Ami] At that time, I saw that, okay, many of us journaling bloggers

00:17:56.784 --> 00:17:59.953
[Ami] were transitioning to making lifestyle content.

00:17:59.953 --> 00:18:03.999
[Ami] This was partly because it was a popular trend,

00:18:03.999 --> 00:18:09.588
[Ami] and partly because its commercial value was higher. Back then, you could get sponsorships for lifestyle content.

00:18:09.630 --> 00:18:11.173
[Ray] What year was that?

00:18:11.215 --> 00:18:13.383
[Ami] It should be around 2018, 2019.

00:18:13.425 --> 00:18:18.472
[Ray] 2018, 2019... when you started creating content, for example, nowadays when many people mention content creation,

00:18:18.472 --> 00:18:20.599
[Ray] the first goal is "I want to make money in the future."

00:18:20.724 --> 00:18:21.225
[Ami] Mmm.

00:18:21.266 --> 00:18:25.729
[Ray] When you first started, was your master plan to make money from this?

00:18:25.979 --> 00:18:30.359
[Ami] Not at all back then. In the beginning, everyone was probably doing it for the love of it.

00:18:30.359 --> 00:18:34.488
[Ami] It's because you didn't see its commercial value. It was very early back then.

00:18:34.488 --> 00:18:38.992
[Ami] People probably just thought, "I just like it, I just like making videos,"

00:18:38.992 --> 00:18:44.289
[Ami] "and I just want to share my life," or get some resonance,

00:18:44.289 --> 00:18:47.709
[Ami] or share some of my life experiences with everyone.

00:18:47.709 --> 00:18:53.006
[Ami] But back then, I can say that for the earliest batch of domestic vloggers,

00:18:53.006 --> 00:18:59.054
[Ami] they probably never thought about how to monetize it. You'd just be doing it, and suddenly a sponsor

00:18:59.054 --> 00:19:04.017
[Ami] would show up, and you'd realize, "Oh, you can actually make money doing this." But then you might...

00:19:04.017 --> 00:19:06.979
[Ami] I felt it was just monetizing through content,

00:19:06.979 --> 00:19:11.066
[Ami] or rather, it felt a bit like something you were forced into.

00:19:11.066 --> 00:19:16.405
[Ami] You have to stick an annoying ad on your content, which wasn't something I really wanted to do.

00:19:16.405 --> 00:19:18.282
[Ami] So at that time, I saw that, okay, there were...

00:19:18.282 --> 00:19:23.245
[Ami] ...journaling bloggers who switched to making lifestyle vlogs, and at the same time,

00:19:23.245 --> 00:19:26.915
[Ami] they didn't give up journaling. They turned journaling into their own brand.

00:19:26.957 --> 00:19:30.002
[Ami] Then I discovered this path they were taking, and I thought, "Hey,

00:19:30.002 --> 00:19:33.088
[Ami] could I also go down this road?"

00:19:33.088 --> 00:19:39.303
[Ami] Because I didn't particularly want to insert sponsored content into my videos.

00:19:39.303 --> 00:19:43.599
[Ami] So I thought, maybe I could also develop some of my own, so to speak...

00:19:43.599 --> 00:19:49.188
[Ami] Back then, nobody called it a "side hustle." It was just developing my own little brand, or making my own little things,

00:19:49.188 --> 00:19:52.524
[Ami] and that's the reason why I started.

00:19:52.566 --> 00:19:56.653
[Ray] Mmm, so when you were about to quit, did this side hustle...

00:19:56.653 --> 00:19:58.780
[Ray] did your content creation bring you any income?

00:19:58.780 --> 00:20:04.119
[Ami] Mmm, yes, it did. But at that time, I can only say it wasn't enough to support me.

00:20:04.119 --> 00:20:10.292
[Ami] To say, okay, I'll rely completely on this. I didn't have the courage to completely abandon my career plan

00:20:10.292 --> 00:20:15.339
[Ami] to pursue what seemed like a not-so-stable career at the time.

00:20:15.339 --> 00:20:20.719
[Ami] Because I thought, okay, since I've joined such a big company, I already have this endorsement on my resume.

00:20:20.719 --> 00:20:23.722
[Ami] I can't waste it. I struggled here for a year, I...

00:20:23.764 --> 00:20:25.349
[Ray] So you still had some illusions.

00:20:25.432 --> 00:20:28.852
[Ami] Right, right, right. I thought, my CV finally looks so good,

00:20:28.852 --> 00:20:34.024
[Ami] to just give up my career like this, I felt it wasn't very... very...

00:20:34.024 --> 00:20:35.817
[Ami] ...very appropriate.

00:20:35.817 --> 00:20:41.365
[Ami] Or rather, the input-output ratio... I had already invested so much, I didn't want it to become a sunk cost.

00:20:41.365 --> 00:20:42.783
[Ami] ...to become a sunk cost.

00:20:42.783 --> 00:20:48.747
[Ami] So at that time, I thought, okay, I have to do both. I told myself I couldn't give up either side. It was a bit greedy, I...

00:20:48.747 --> 00:20:53.001
[Ami] But I think it was also because neither side gave me enough security.

00:20:53.001 --> 00:20:55.462
[Ami] So I had to do both at the same time,

00:20:55.462 --> 00:20:59.341
[Ami] in order to reach what felt like a relatively safe state.

00:20:59.383 --> 00:21:04.513
[Ray] Mmm, I find that you're a very observant person, a very mindful person.

00:21:04.513 --> 00:21:09.434
[Ray] So, in your second job, in that environment full of big bad wolves,

00:21:09.434 --> 00:21:13.146
[Ray] did you observe anything new, or learn anything new?

00:21:13.188 --> 00:21:16.608
[Ami] Mmm, I think, looking back now,

00:21:16.608 --> 00:21:21.279
[Ami] a sentence that has always stayed with me is something my boss at the time told me.

00:21:21.279 --> 00:21:24.950
[Ami] It was, "In work, know what's urgent and important; in life, know who's close and who's distant."

00:21:24.950 --> 00:21:29.705
[Ami] When he first interviewed me, he told me, he said, "Ami, when it comes to work and life,"

00:21:29.705 --> 00:21:32.499
[Ami] "it's really just these eight characters. When you're doing things,"

00:21:32.499 --> 00:21:38.171
[Ami] "you have to be clear about your priorities. In dealing with people, you also have to distinguish,"

00:21:38.171 --> 00:21:44.428
[Ami] "whether you should have a deep relationship with them, or just be acquaintances. This is something you have to learn in work, and in life."

00:21:44.428 --> 00:21:46.638
[Ami] "This eight-character truth you must learn."

00:21:46.638 --> 00:21:47.472
[Ami] That's what he told me.

00:21:47.514 --> 00:21:52.227
[Ami] Although I felt the whole work environment was very oppressive, those eight words he gave me,

00:21:52.227 --> 00:21:57.065
[Ami] I feel they have become a maxim in my life.

00:21:57.065 --> 00:21:58.317
[Ami] I should put it that way.

00:21:58.358 --> 00:22:03.530
[Ray] Okay, so first, was this the same boss you were writing the report for late at night?

00:22:03.530 --> 00:22:04.531
[Ray] Was it the same person?

00:22:04.990 --> 00:22:07.534
[Ami] Kind of, hahaha, kind of.

00:22:08.535 --> 00:22:12.748
[Ray] So then I want to ask you, do you have an example, either from that job back then or later on,

00:22:12.748 --> 00:22:16.418
[Ray] of how those eight words have guided your actions?

00:22:16.877 --> 00:22:22.799
[Ami] I think in the "getting things done" part, I have more practical experience.

00:22:22.799 --> 00:22:27.346
[Ami] Because whether it was in my previous job, the one I mentioned,

00:22:27.346 --> 00:22:33.226
[Ami] there were always so, so many things to do. The bosses' priorities were also very important.

00:22:33.226 --> 00:22:37.981
[Ami] You have to rank them according to the senior boss, the junior boss, right?

00:22:37.981 --> 00:22:41.902
[Ami] Only then can you set your own priorities correctly.

00:22:41.902 --> 00:22:46.114
[Ami] And secondly, for example, now in my so-called freelance career,

00:22:46.114 --> 00:22:51.495
[Ami] my freelance work involves quite a lot because I'm still doing content creation

00:22:51.495 --> 00:22:56.500
[Ami] and also running the brand. So when many things come up at the same time,

00:22:56.500 --> 00:23:01.630
[Ami] for us J-type personalities, we love to prioritize things.

00:23:01.630 --> 00:23:07.552
[Ami] For example, you classify things as urgent or not urgent, or important and not important. And sometimes,

00:23:07.594 --> 00:23:10.055
[Ami] for instance, when a brand is chasing you for a draft,

00:23:10.097 --> 00:23:13.809
[Ami] okay, you have to put that in the "important and urgent" category.

00:23:13.809 --> 00:23:18.730
[Ami] As for coordinating with the factory for the stationery products, I think that can be pushed back a bit.

00:23:18.730 --> 00:23:24.569
[Ami] So this is something that I feel, in my work, both in that situation back then and my current situation,

00:23:24.569 --> 00:23:29.908
[Ami] has been a very important lesson I've learned about time management.

00:23:29.908 --> 00:23:35.038
[Ami] Without those eight words, everything would probably be a chaotic mess, and I'd be very lost.

00:23:35.122 --> 00:23:39.751
[Ray] Mmm, so that's why you later made a lot of content about time management, efficiency, including Notion

00:23:39.876 --> 00:23:44.714
[Ray] tutorials. It all originated from those eight words from your boss.

00:23:44.798 --> 00:23:44.840
[Ami] The inspiration, yes.

00:23:44.881 --> 00:23:46.258
[Ray] Right, right, that inspiration.

00:23:46.258 --> 00:23:51.930
[Ray] You mentioned sponsors chasing you for drafts. If a sponsor chased me for a draft, I would just...

00:23:51.930 --> 00:23:53.473
[Ami] Hahaha, it's not important.

00:23:53.515 --> 00:23:56.560
[Ray] Right, I'd just delete, delete. "Don't ever contact me again."

00:23:56.560 --> 00:23:59.020
[Ray] So how did you tell your boss when you quit?

00:23:59.020 --> 00:24:00.188
[Ray] Or was it a "nobody cares" situation?

00:24:01.106 --> 00:24:07.154
[Ami] I think in my eyes, everyone should have been very sad to lose a capable underling like me.

00:24:07.154 --> 00:24:11.324
[Ami] But in his eyes, it was probably "nobody cares." For him,

00:24:11.324 --> 00:24:16.163
[Ami] I think for a big company, you're very replaceable.

00:24:16.163 --> 00:24:20.250
[Ami] But in my own life, okay, I am very important.

00:24:20.250 --> 00:24:24.045
[Ami] So back then, I was very straightforward with my boss. I just told him,

00:24:24.045 --> 00:24:26.631
[Ami] "I'm planning to pursue further studies."

00:24:26.631 --> 00:24:30.594
[Ami] "So I hope to leave the workforce for a while."

00:24:30.594 --> 00:24:32.387
[Ami] Although I had no intention of studying,

00:24:32.387 --> 00:24:38.268
[Ami] I submitted my resignation with this rather polite and formal reason.

00:24:38.268 --> 00:24:42.314
[Ami] Of course, later on, I did think about whether to actually continue my studies,

00:24:42.314 --> 00:24:45.775
[Ami] but I felt that if I brought it up with my boss under this pretense,

00:24:45.775 --> 00:24:48.820
[Ami] it might be more easily accepted.

00:24:48.820 --> 00:24:49.779
[Ami] From my perspective.

00:24:49.863 --> 00:24:52.240
[Ray] So the boss just said, "Okay, you can go then"?

00:24:52.240 --> 00:24:53.491
[Ray] No attempt to keep you?

00:24:53.575 --> 00:24:56.620
[Ami] He did try to keep me, but I felt that...

00:24:56.620 --> 00:25:01.666
[Ami] the terms he offered to make me stay weren't attractive enough for me.

00:25:01.708 --> 00:25:03.710
[Ray] So he did make you a counteroffer.

00:25:03.835 --> 00:25:09.174
[Ami] Yes, and he probably felt that after making the offer, I couldn't...

00:25:09.174 --> 00:25:15.055
[Ami] when I didn't give him a "yes," he might have said, "Oh, how about you stay a few more months

00:25:15.055 --> 00:25:16.932
[Ami] and leave after you get the bonus."

00:25:17.140 --> 00:25:18.642
[Ami] Okay, I compromised on that one.

00:25:18.767 --> 00:25:19.559
[Ray] Hahahaha.

00:25:19.601 --> 00:25:23.772
[Ami] So this... because at the time, my boss... when I first joined,

00:25:23.772 --> 00:25:28.693
[Ami] I didn't know how much the company's bonus would be, because based on my experience at the previous startup,

00:25:28.693 --> 00:25:29.903
[Ami] I thought it wouldn't be much.

00:25:29.903 --> 00:25:32.614
[Ami] But after talking with my boss, I found out, oh, it's quite a lot.

00:25:32.614 --> 00:25:38.245
[Ami] And okay, I said, "Then I'll hang in there for a few more months." And after hanging on for a few more months, I officially left that company.

00:25:38.286 --> 00:25:43.291
[Ray] Mmm, so after you left, on the day you left, did you feel a sense of relief,

00:25:43.291 --> 00:25:44.793
[Ray] or how did you feel?

00:25:45.001 --> 00:25:49.214
[Ami] Mmm, to be honest, I actually felt a bit of regret at the time.

00:25:49.214 --> 00:25:54.844
[Ami] Yes. I think with everything, at the moment you actually leave, you might feel a little...

00:25:54.844 --> 00:25:57.138
[Ami] "Am I really leaving this company?"

00:25:57.138 --> 00:26:00.225
[Ami] To be honest, the benefits were pretty good. Every day in Central,

00:26:00.225 --> 00:26:06.064
[Ami] you could get a meal with four dishes and a soup for just ten dollars. I thought it was great in every aspect.

00:26:06.064 --> 00:26:09.526
[Ami] At the time, I thought to myself, "If you really quit, will you regret it?"

00:26:09.526 --> 00:26:12.195
[Ami] But I also talked to my family about it. I said,

00:26:12.195 --> 00:26:17.784
[Ami] "What if I end up feeling I made the wrong decision?" And my mom told me,

00:26:17.784 --> 00:26:19.911
[Ami] "Since you've made the decision, don't regret it."

00:26:19.911 --> 00:26:25.792
[Ami] She said the key is, once you've decided on something, don't look back.

00:26:25.792 --> 00:26:29.421
[Ami] So when she told me that, I thought, "Okay, I've already submitted my resignation.

00:26:29.421 --> 00:26:31.464
[Ami] I can't possibly ask my boss for it back."

00:26:31.464 --> 00:26:36.595
[Ami] So, how should I put it... I had regrets, but I also felt a strong sense of liberation.

00:26:36.678 --> 00:26:39.764
[Ami] I still have a vivid memory of the day I left.

00:26:39.764 --> 00:26:45.312
[Ami] There was a woman in administration, probably much older than me,

00:26:45.312 --> 00:26:49.691
[Ami] and she left me a message. She said that although we hadn't known each other for long,

00:26:49.691 --> 00:26:55.071
[Ami] she thought I was a very reliable worker. That actually gave me a lot of confidence.

00:26:55.071 --> 00:26:59.826
[Ami] I thought, "Okay, if the lady from admin and others give me this kind of feedback,

00:26:59.826 --> 00:27:05.790
[Ami] I believe finding my next job won't be too difficult."

00:27:05.790 --> 00:27:10.128
[Ami] So at that moment, I just thought, "Okay, what's done is done. We'll meet again somewhere down the line."

00:27:10.211 --> 00:27:14.799
[Ray] So at that point, had you already found a new job, or not?

00:27:14.924 --> 00:27:18.386
[Ami] No, not at all. It was a complete... I guess you could call it "quitting without a backup plan."

00:27:18.386 --> 00:27:18.636
[Ami] Right.

00:27:18.720 --> 00:27:20.680
[Ray] Okay, and what did you do next?

00:27:20.847 --> 00:27:24.392
[Ami] Okay, the first thing I did next was to start exercising.

00:27:24.392 --> 00:27:27.562
[Ami] Because I felt that in such a high-pressure environment,

00:27:27.562 --> 00:27:30.482
[Ami] I really had no time to take care of my physical health.

00:27:30.482 --> 00:27:35.904
[Ami] So, okay, the first thing I did was get a monthly gym membership.

00:27:35.904 --> 00:27:40.867
[Ami] Whether I would stick with it was another matter, but I got the membership first.

00:27:40.867 --> 00:27:44.037
[Ami] And then I started pursuing some things I enjoy.

00:27:44.037 --> 00:27:49.084
[Ami] I spent about two-plus months doing everything I wanted to do,

00:27:49.084 --> 00:27:53.380
[Ami] for example, I went dancing, and I took some drawing classes for a while.

00:27:53.380 --> 00:27:58.385
[Ami] I also started planning what I wanted to do in the future,

00:27:58.385 --> 00:28:04.641
[Ami] whether to continue working or just take a break for a while, making a life plan like that.

00:28:04.641 --> 00:28:04.682
[Ami] .

00:28:04.724 --> 00:28:09.312
[Ray] Hmm, and then what? Did you start looking for another job, or were you hesitant,

00:28:09.312 --> 00:28:12.065
[Ray] thinking... "Should I even look for a job?"

00:28:12.357 --> 00:28:18.113
[Ami] Hmm, I was actually very hesitant at the time, because my side hustle was doing okay,

00:28:18.113 --> 00:28:21.574
[Ami] but I didn't feel it was enough to be a complete safety net for me.

00:28:21.616 --> 00:28:22.867
[Ray] What do you mean by "doing okay"?

00:28:22.867 --> 00:28:25.453
[Ray] Like you started selling things, or you were getting ad deals?

00:28:25.870 --> 00:28:30.291
[Ami] Both. It started turning a profit. I felt the profit was not bad.

00:28:30.291 --> 00:28:35.672
[Ami] It could match my previous salary, the one from the job I had just quit.

00:28:35.672 --> 00:28:40.301
[Ami] Secondly, regarding ad deals, advertisers would come along irregularly.

00:28:40.301 --> 00:28:45.640
[Ami] But because my last job was so busy, I wasn't updating consistently, so the flow was very unstable.

00:28:45.640 --> 00:28:50.478
[Ami] So, and at the same time, more and more people were becoming content creators.

00:28:50.478 --> 00:28:53.398
[Ami] So I felt that the competition in social media was getting intense.

00:28:53.398 --> 00:28:56.609
[Ami] And my cultural and creative side hustle was stable,

00:28:56.609 --> 00:29:01.948
[Ami] but I didn't think it had reached a level where it could provide me with a safety net.

00:29:01.948 --> 00:29:04.117
[Ami] So I was really hesitating.

00:29:04.117 --> 00:29:09.998
[Ami] During that time, I thought, "Okay, I'll go home for a bit." So I went back to Shanghai for a trip.

00:29:09.998 --> 00:29:15.879
[Ami] And it just so happened to be during the pandemic, so it wasn't particularly easy to find a job.

00:29:15.879 --> 00:29:20.967
[Ami] So I was casually sending out applications, seeing if any suitable opportunities came up. If they did,

00:29:20.967 --> 00:29:23.428
[Ami] I would decide on my next step then.

00:29:23.470 --> 00:29:25.847
[Ray] Hmm, so did you find a job in the end?

00:29:25.847 --> 00:29:31.352
[Ami] Hmm, okay, it was about half a year later. Because of the pandemic, I found it wasn't easy to find one.

00:29:31.352 --> 00:29:36.900
[Ami] And secondly, even when I found something, I didn't think it was particularly good or appealing to me.

00:29:36.900 --> 00:29:41.779
[Ami] Because I felt that, okay, if I were to re-enter the workforce, that decision would be crucial.

00:29:41.779 --> 00:29:47.702
[Ami] Because I believed that whatever job I took then, I would probably be doing it for a long time. I suppose

00:29:47.702 --> 00:29:50.288
[Ami] I would stay for a long time.

00:29:50.330 --> 00:29:54.667
[Ami] So the choice of company was very important to me.

00:29:54.667 --> 00:29:59.088
[Ami] I wouldn't just jump into a company simply because it offered a high salary.

00:29:59.088 --> 00:30:04.803
[Ami] So I waited for a long time, and then, quite coincidentally, a foreign investment bank in Shanghai reached out to me.

00:30:04.803 --> 00:30:10.225
[Ami] And I thought, "Okay, I've worked at a Chinese investment bank. Maybe I should try a foreign one?"

00:30:10.308 --> 00:30:15.313
[Ami] Because I thought, after all, the work environments should be quite different. Regardless of whether I stayed for a long time

00:30:15.313 --> 00:30:17.690
[Ami] or quit halfway through,

00:30:17.690 --> 00:30:22.529
[Ami] it would be an opportunity to enrich my life experience.

00:30:22.529 --> 00:30:26.574
[Ami] So I accepted the offer from that... uh, foreign investment bank in Shanghai.

00:30:26.658 --> 00:30:29.744
[Ray] So you went in with the intention of a long-term commitment.

00:30:29.786 --> 00:30:30.578
[Ami] Right, yes.

00:30:30.620 --> 00:30:33.957
[Ray] But how did you end up leaving that job then?

00:30:34.040 --> 00:30:36.918
[Ami] Okay, this is actually... quite funny.

00:30:36.918 --> 00:30:41.506
[Ami] At this foreign investment bank, I was also doing PR-related work,

00:30:41.506 --> 00:30:46.928
[Ami] but it was more internal. I was responsible for promoting the company's own culture.

00:30:46.928 --> 00:30:48.429
[Ami] And in that company,

00:30:48.429 --> 00:30:53.351
[Ami] you know how foreign companies have a lot of what we call a "purpose," a company's

00:30:53.351 --> 00:30:56.104
[Ami] vision, what its vision is.

00:30:56.104 --> 00:30:57.897
[Ami] So my job at the time

00:30:57.897 --> 00:31:03.611
[Ami] was to promote the company's purpose, its vision, and to communicate it to our internal

00:31:03.611 --> 00:31:04.779
[Ami] employees.

00:31:04.821 --> 00:31:06.823
[Ray] Basically, brainwashing everyone, uh, yeah.

00:31:06.823 --> 00:31:07.657
[Ray] (Laughs)

00:31:07.740 --> 00:31:12.996
[Ami] Right, right. Brainwashing people internally, brainwashing people externally, saying our company has this grand vision.

00:31:12.996 --> 00:31:17.417
[Ami] And this thing itself, I felt, was quite, how should I say, quite broad.

00:31:17.417 --> 00:31:23.089
[Ami] It wasn't very specific about, okay, what we would do. That was for the business departments

00:31:23.089 --> 00:31:24.090
[Ami] to pursue.

00:31:24.090 --> 00:31:27.969
[Ami] Our main job was more to build the corporate culture, or the corporate image.

00:31:27.969 --> 00:31:33.349
[Ami] And at the time, our CEO, he had this promotional video that I...

00:31:33.349 --> 00:31:34.726
[Ami] I had to watch it over and over again,

00:31:34.726 --> 00:31:39.731
[Ami] because I had to understand the CEO's interpretation of this vision, this purpose,

00:31:39.731 --> 00:31:44.277
[Ami] so that I could create my promotional plan.

00:31:44.277 --> 00:31:48.698
[Ami] And I kept watching it, and there was one line in it

00:31:48.698 --> 00:31:50.867
[Ami] that left a very deep impression on me.

00:31:50.867 --> 00:31:54.037
[Ami] It said that everyone should have their own purpose. This purpose

00:31:54.162 --> 00:31:59.751
[Ami] is the first thing you want to do when you wake up and open your eyes every morning. And I actually felt very,

00:31:59.751 --> 00:32:02.879
[Ami] very... it was like a shock.

00:32:02.879 --> 00:32:06.466
[Ami] I was helping the company promote its purpose every day,

00:32:06.466 --> 00:32:08.259
[Ami] but what was my own purpose?

00:32:08.301 --> 00:32:11.095
[Ami] And the first thing I thought about when I woke up every morning was, "Oh,

00:32:11.095 --> 00:32:14.891
[Ami] my to-do list for the company today is one, two, three."

00:32:14.891 --> 00:32:17.018
[Ami] So what is the purpose of my own life?

00:32:17.018 --> 00:32:21.272
[Ami] It's like, I was supposed to be promoting the company's purpose to everyone,

00:32:21.272 --> 00:32:25.443
[Ami] but instead, it suddenly started making me doubt myself.

00:32:25.443 --> 00:32:28.154
[Ami] And it also started making me think, "Okay,

00:32:28.154 --> 00:32:32.659
[Ami] maybe I shouldn't be helping others think about their purpose anymore,

00:32:32.659 --> 00:32:36.371
[Ami] but should instead look at what I truly want."

00:32:36.371 --> 00:32:39.624
[Ami] So this was quite... a bit... a bit funny for me.

00:32:39.624 --> 00:32:43.002
[Ami] You were originally supposed to be doing this promotion for the company,

00:32:43.002 --> 00:32:46.798
[Ami] but it ended up making you question yourself.

00:32:46.881 --> 00:32:49.717
[Ray] So you started treating yourself like a company.

00:32:49.884 --> 00:32:55.390
[Ami] Yes. Because when you're in that position, I think it's hard to think from an individual's

00:32:55.390 --> 00:32:59.727
[Ami] paradigm. Okay, I had to think on a corporate level,

00:32:59.727 --> 00:33:04.899
[Ami] about how to promote its philosophy and how to implement it with the people below.

00:33:04.899 --> 00:33:09.654
[Ami] For instance, I had to write various internal letters. We would write these letters in the CEO's

00:33:09.737 --> 00:33:14.534
[Ami] name and send them to all our colleagues internally.

00:33:14.534 --> 00:33:19.288
[Ami] You have to think from a leader's perspective. I found that I had lost... lost myself.

00:33:19.288 --> 00:33:23.710
[Ami] It felt like I had no purpose of my own, but I was still trying to sell a dream to others.

00:33:23.835 --> 00:33:24.794
[Ray] Hey, I find this very interesting.

00:33:24.836 --> 00:33:30.466
[Ray] You often find yourself in this process of self-reflection, thinking, "Hey,

00:33:30.466 --> 00:33:34.011
[Ray] what is my purpose, what are all these things to me?"

00:33:34.011 --> 00:33:38.975
[Ray] And it seems like going to work is always a difficult thing for you to endure.

00:33:38.975 --> 00:33:40.768
[Ray] Why is that?

00:33:40.768 --> 00:33:45.523
[Ray] You're like this, but your colleagues, especially those your age, aren't they different?

00:33:45.523 --> 00:33:48.317
[Ray] Why do you think you're different from them?

00:33:48.943 --> 00:33:53.281
[Ami] I think it might be because I have an option, an option to exit.

00:33:53.281 --> 00:33:58.369
[Ami] Some people... well, actually, I think many of my colleagues,

00:33:58.369 --> 00:34:00.288
[Ami] or my peers,

00:34:00.288 --> 00:34:05.960
[Ami] might see work as their life's mission, or their main quest.

00:34:05.960 --> 00:34:11.591
[Ami] But for me, I've created side quests in my life, like my side hustle, or my social media.

00:34:11.591 --> 00:34:17.430
[Ami] I know that, okay, if I don't do this main quest, I have many other side quests I can do.

00:34:17.430 --> 00:34:23.061
[Ami] It's not like, okay, when I can only see this one main path in my life, I have no choice but to move forward.

00:34:23.061 --> 00:34:26.898
[Ami] But if I have these side quests, or these forks in the road,

00:34:26.898 --> 00:34:32.361
[Ami] then I'll start to think, "Okay, can I not do this main quest and just do the side quests?"

00:34:32.361 --> 00:34:37.283
[Ami] In this world full of NPCs, I could just wander around and not do the main quest.

00:34:37.283 --> 00:34:40.495
[Ami] I don't want to reach a so-called predetermined ending.

00:34:40.495 --> 00:34:46.334
[Ami] So, could I perhaps develop some of my own little side stories, or go in a different direction?

00:34:46.334 --> 00:34:50.922
[Ami] I think that's one area where I might be different from others.

00:34:50.922 --> 00:34:56.844
[Ami] I have the option to quit, instead of just blindly pushing forward with everyone else.

00:34:57.220 --> 00:34:58.387
[Ray] What you said is actually very interesting.

00:34:58.387 --> 00:35:02.683
[Ray] Last night at dinner, I was with Remy from our community, who is also my editor.

00:35:02.683 --> 00:35:08.397
[Ray] We've known each other for a long time, and we were talking about the future of Yikou Xin Fan, and some monetization methods.

00:35:08.397 --> 00:35:14.654
[Ray] And she said, "Ray, I've noticed you seem to have ten thousand ways to make money."

00:35:14.654 --> 00:35:18.407
[Ray] It's like you have countless ways to earn money, but if you have a job,

00:35:18.407 --> 00:35:20.827
[Ray] it seems there's only one way.

00:35:20.827 --> 00:35:25.039
[Ray] So I think this is also a very important point about side hustles. It tells you

00:35:25.039 --> 00:35:28.376
[Ray] that your so-called main quest in life isn't the only one.

00:35:28.376 --> 00:35:34.340
[Ray] You actually have many of these side plots, which can be even richer than the main story,

00:35:34.340 --> 00:35:35.508
[Ray] these storylines.

00:35:35.508 --> 00:35:40.179
[Ray] So I think you are a perfect example of this.

00:35:40.304 --> 00:35:46.018
[Ray] When everyone is thinking, "My only rice bowl is what this company gives me," then you might...

00:35:46.018 --> 00:35:50.481
[Ray] it's like having blinders put on you, like a donkey turning a mill, right?

00:35:50.481 --> 00:35:52.733
[Ray] This fits the theme perfectly, right?

00:35:52.733 --> 00:35:58.531
[Ray] It's like having blinders on. They block your sides, and you can only go forward,

00:35:58.531 --> 00:35:59.198
[Ray] and that's it.

00:35:59.282 --> 00:36:04.412
[Ray] You think you're moving forward, but when you're turning the mill, you're just going in circles.

00:36:04.412 --> 00:36:06.164
[Ray] You're not getting sidetracked.

00:36:06.164 --> 00:36:09.083
[Ray] Actually, sometimes when you get sidetracked, when you start a side hustle,

00:36:09.083 --> 00:36:15.631
[Ray] you're actually creating the first of your ten thousand ways to make money.

00:36:15.631 --> 00:36:21.470
[Ray] I think this is very important. So I still encourage everyone—since the theme of this episode is about side hustles—

00:36:21.470 --> 00:36:23.347
[Ray] to actively pursue side hustles.

00:36:23.347 --> 00:36:26.767
[Ray] So why did you decide to leave that place too?

00:36:26.767 --> 00:36:29.186
[Ray] Was there a specific moment, a trigger?

00:36:29.270 --> 00:36:33.983
[Ami] Hmm, that moment was also related to this "purpose." At the time,

00:36:33.983 --> 00:36:39.739
[Ami] the company's leadership changed, and so... they stopped promoting that purpose, you know?

00:36:39.739 --> 00:36:44.702
[Ami] I was really shocked at the time, because I had been at that company for about a year,

00:36:44.702 --> 00:36:46.787
[Ami] and I had spent about ten

00:36:46.787 --> 00:36:52.126
[Ami] or eleven months promoting this purpose. And okay, I had come to understand this purpose. Okay,

00:36:52.126 --> 00:36:55.087
[Ami] I fully understood it, and I knew how to promote it.

00:36:55.087 --> 00:36:59.967
[Ami] Then the leadership changed, and this purpose became a... what do you call it...

00:36:59.967 --> 00:37:05.765
[Ami] a so-called legacy. A legacy that you couldn't touch anymore, because you had to work on something new.

00:37:05.765 --> 00:37:09.518
[Ami] And I thought, "So this purpose can be overturned so easily?"

00:37:09.518 --> 00:37:11.729
[Ami] Then what was I doing for the past ten months?

00:37:11.729 --> 00:37:17.318
[Ami] It went from a sense of self-doubt to an even greater sense of self-doubt.

00:37:17.318 --> 00:37:21.614
[Ami] Both at work—okay, I couldn't find the meaning in my work anymore.

00:37:21.614 --> 00:37:23.991
[Ami] Of course, I think work itself is meaningless.

00:37:23.991 --> 00:37:29.956
[Ami] And I realized, okay, this thing I had put so much effort into was completely overturned.

00:37:29.956 --> 00:37:33.292
[Ami] So what was the point of all my efforts?

00:37:33.334 --> 00:37:37.338
[Ami] And at the same time, I was thinking, "Okay, your purpose is gone,

00:37:37.338 --> 00:37:39.465
[Ami] so where exactly is my purpose?"

00:37:39.465 --> 00:37:44.553
[Ami] After this double blow, I just felt, "Okay, this is meaningless."

00:37:44.553 --> 00:37:50.768
[Ami] My definition for this job became "meaningless." I discovered it was just a huge... a huge...

00:37:50.768 --> 00:37:52.770
[Ami] an amateur operation.

00:37:52.770 --> 00:37:58.401
[Ami] And I thought, here I am, just like an NPC, without a self, without my own opinion.

00:37:58.401 --> 00:38:03.364
[Ami] Just doing whatever you tell me to do, and not doing whatever you tell me not to do, in this... in this... in this...

00:38:03.364 --> 00:38:04.824
[Ami] huge amateur operation.

00:38:04.824 --> 00:38:08.828
[Ami] And I just felt, "Okay, this is meaningless. I don't want to do it." And...

00:38:08.869 --> 00:38:14.667
[Ray] This... what you mentioned reminds me of a scene from a movie, Forrest Gump.

00:38:14.667 --> 00:38:19.088
[Ray] In "Forrest Gump," he runs back and forth, from the East Coast to the West Coast, and back again.

00:38:19.088 --> 00:38:24.260
[Ray] Then one day, he suddenly stops. But by then, he had gathered a lot of followers behind him.

00:38:24.260 --> 00:38:26.804
[Ray] And they ask, "Hey, what should we do now?"

00:38:26.804 --> 00:38:29.557
[Ray] And Forrest Gump? He just went home, leaving them to their own devices.

00:38:29.557 --> 00:38:35.771
[Ray] So, you actually brought up a point that many people might disagree with. You said you believe work is meaningless.

00:38:35.771 --> 00:38:37.815
[Ray] A lot of people would say, "No, no, no, it has meaning."

00:38:37.815 --> 00:38:43.029
[Ray] Actually, a lot of people have debated this with me, argued against it.

00:38:43.029 --> 00:38:48.743
[Ray] Ah, and I think they make a certain amount of sense. They'd say, "Look, my work is noble.

00:38:48.743 --> 00:38:51.662
[Ray] I'm creating value. It's meaningful."

00:38:51.662 --> 00:38:52.997
[Ray] But there's a very important point here,

00:38:52.997 --> 00:38:56.250
[Ray] which is what you just mentioned: whose mission is this mission, really?

00:38:56.250 --> 00:38:59.712
[Ray] It's the company's mission. For example, Elon Musk. You could say his mission

00:38:59.795 --> 00:39:05.468
[Ray] is to advance the adoption of new energy, renewable energy, sustainable energy.

00:39:05.468 --> 00:39:09.805
[Ray] But that doesn't mean that if Elon Musk suddenly says one day, "Okay, this mission is complete,"

00:39:09.805 --> 00:39:14.935
[Ray] or "I'm changing the mission, everyone should go to Mars," or he decides to get into politics now,

00:39:14.935 --> 00:39:15.811
[Ray] what would you do then?

00:39:15.853 --> 00:39:18.731
[Ray] It's like everyone was following Forrest Gump,

00:39:18.731 --> 00:39:22.526
[Ray] you have a mission, or what you imagine is your mission, but suddenly he stops,

00:39:22.526 --> 00:39:25.029
[Ray] when he changes, what do you do?

00:39:25.029 --> 00:39:27.406
[Ray] So someone else's mission is always someone else's mission.

00:39:27.448 --> 00:39:32.578
[Ray] Right. So when you find that you can't find your own mission, then you just... you just quit.

00:39:32.578 --> 00:39:33.245
[Ray] You just quit.

00:39:33.329 --> 00:39:36.373
[Ray] So when you quit, did you tell your boss? What was their reaction?

00:39:36.373 --> 00:39:38.250
[Ray] Or did the new boss not care?

00:39:38.542 --> 00:39:44.256
[Ami] Um, my boss at the time did try to get me to stay. Every time, my boss would... I would...

00:39:44.256 --> 00:39:49.678
[Ami] I don't know if this is just a workplace formality, something people do for show, but my boss would say,

00:39:49.678 --> 00:39:55.643
[Ami] he asked for my reason for leaving, but at that point, I couldn't really use the excuse of going back to school again.

00:39:55.643 --> 00:40:01.107
[Ami] So I just said I might want to do my own thing. Then my boss didn't really have any...

00:40:01.107 --> 00:40:02.775
[Ami] ...way to convince me to stay.

00:40:02.817 --> 00:40:04.860
[Ray] Did they, uh, give you a counter-offer?

00:40:04.944 --> 00:40:05.945
[Ami] They did.

00:40:05.945 --> 00:40:10.324
[Ray] Ah, so you were just wondering if in the workplace, uh, people are just putting on a show.

00:40:10.324 --> 00:40:12.910
[Ray] There's one way to tell: whether they offer you more money.

00:40:12.952 --> 00:40:13.119
[Ami] Oh.

00:40:13.160 --> 00:40:14.161
[Ami] (Laughs)

00:40:14.245 --> 00:40:17.414
[Ray] It's that simple. If they offer you more money, then it's real.

00:40:17.414 --> 00:40:22.962
[Ray] If not, and they just say, "Oh, Ami is so great, so reliable. Your leaving is a huge loss for the company,"

00:40:22.962 --> 00:40:26.507
[Ray] but, uh, don't mention money at all, then it's just a courtesy.

00:40:26.507 --> 00:40:28.008
[Ray] So, did you hesitate?

00:40:28.634 --> 00:40:34.598
[Ami] This time, I actually didn't hesitate at all because, because what was different from my last job was that,

00:40:34.598 --> 00:40:36.142
[Ami] I felt like I had seen through it all.

00:40:36.183 --> 00:40:36.809
[Ray] Seen through it all.

00:40:36.851 --> 00:40:41.814
[Ami] I just felt that this, this workplace environment, firstly, it wasn't for me,

00:40:41.814 --> 00:40:45.484
[Ami] and secondly, I felt it couldn't bring me any value.

00:40:45.484 --> 00:40:51.115
[Ami] I realized, OK, I don't want to play this game anymore. I want to play my own game.

00:40:51.115 --> 00:40:56.245
[Ami] That was my third job. From quitting until now, I have absolutely no regrets.

00:40:56.245 --> 00:41:01.917
[Ami] I'm even glad I made that decision back then.

00:41:02.001 --> 00:41:04.795
[Ray] Hmm, so back then, when we first talked,

00:41:04.795 --> 00:41:10.509
[Ray] you said it was a gap year, and the year was almost over, and you said you'd continue the gap.

00:41:10.509 --> 00:41:15.389
[Ray] But I always felt you were holding back, that you had an option, like maybe one day if I can't make it,

00:41:15.389 --> 00:41:16.849
[Ray] I can still go back to a job.

00:41:16.849 --> 00:41:18.642
[Ray] Do you still have that thought now?

00:41:18.684 --> 00:41:20.060
[Ami] Uh, not at all.

00:41:20.060 --> 00:41:20.853
[Ray] Not at all.

00:41:20.895 --> 00:41:26.942
[Ray] Even if, say, you feel like... for example, if your self-media thing doesn't work out,

00:41:26.942 --> 00:41:28.944
[Ray] your, OK, knock on the wood,

00:41:28.944 --> 00:41:33.324
[Ray] your own startup project fails, even in that situation, it's not possible?

00:41:33.490 --> 00:41:35.034
[Ami] I don't think so.

00:41:35.034 --> 00:41:39.997
[Ami] I think if it really comes to that, I should have saved up a certain amount, a certain retirement fund.

00:41:40.039 --> 00:41:45.002
[Ray] OK, OK. I think for many people who leave the corporate world, there's a desensitization,

00:41:45.002 --> 00:41:48.839
[Ray] or rather, a withdrawal period. There's a phase like that.

00:41:48.839 --> 00:41:51.175
[Ray] For some it's longer, for some it's shorter.

00:41:51.175 --> 00:41:56.013
[Ray] For me, it was about a week, and after that, I could never go back.

00:41:56.013 --> 00:42:02.019
[Ray] I've shared this saying before: once you've tasted freedom, right,

00:42:02.019 --> 00:42:04.271
[Ray] tasted it, you can never go back.

00:42:04.271 --> 00:42:08.108
[Ray] Because when you work a job, you realize you're just locking yourself in a cage.

00:42:08.150 --> 00:42:10.653
[Ray] OK, let's talk about your startup.

00:42:10.653 --> 00:42:16.325
[Ray] You said you started making money from a side hustle during your second job. What were you selling back then?

00:42:16.450 --> 00:42:21.372
[Ami] I was selling some... Let me start with my first product.

00:42:21.372 --> 00:42:25.543
[Ami] In the very beginning, I had no concept of starting a business at all.

00:42:25.543 --> 00:42:31.090
[Ami] I just had an idea and wanted to turn it into a product. And I brought that product today.

00:42:31.090 --> 00:42:34.051
[Ami] It's this light-up calendar.

00:42:34.051 --> 00:42:37.471
[Ami] I'm not sure if you all have seen this on your feeds before. This one.

00:42:37.763 --> 00:42:38.556
[Ray] I think I've come across it.

00:42:38.597 --> 00:42:40.975
[Ami] What I wanted to make at the time was...

00:42:40.975 --> 00:42:45.104
[Ami] I just felt that everyone's calendars were very boring, and I thought, hey,

00:42:45.104 --> 00:42:50.401
[Ami] could I make one that lights up, and at the same time, you can also... it actually has a pen, which I didn't bring today,

00:42:50.401 --> 00:42:52.778
[Ami] ...you can write on it, and you can also erase it.

00:42:52.778 --> 00:42:56.365
[Ami] That way, I thought, OK, I can bring it to the office, and I'd have a, OK,

00:42:56.615 --> 00:43:01.704
[Ami] a ritual for starting work. I'd light it up to show I'm at my desk, and when I get off work, I'd turn it off

00:43:01.704 --> 00:43:04.123
[Ami] to show I'm off the clock, don't come looking for me.

00:43:04.123 --> 00:43:05.916
[Ami] It was a feeling like that.

00:43:05.916 --> 00:43:10.170
[Ami] Then I thought, hey, isn't it usually the case that when you think of a product,

00:43:10.170 --> 00:43:14.842
[Ami] you search online and find that everyone's already made it? And that's very, very discouraging.

00:43:14.842 --> 00:43:17.469
[Ami] But at that time, I went online and searched.

00:43:17.469 --> 00:43:22.641
[Ami] I searched for this thing called an acrylic calendar, and found that no one had made it.

00:43:22.641 --> 00:43:27.021
[Ami] And even this format of being able to write on it, no one had done that either.

00:43:27.062 --> 00:43:32.151
[Ami] Then, right away, during a break at work, I suddenly got a shot of adrenaline.

00:43:32.151 --> 00:43:33.319
[Ami] I told myself I had to make it.

00:43:33.319 --> 00:43:36.614
[Ami] Then I thought, hey, maybe I can make a sample first?

00:43:36.614 --> 00:43:42.036
[Ami] Because at the time, I knew nothing about the whole product creation process. So I went on, probably Taobao,

00:43:42.036 --> 00:43:47.791
[Ami] and I asked them to help me. I got a blank piece of acrylic board because I needed to test if it could be written on,

00:43:47.791 --> 00:43:50.127
[Ami] if it could be erased, and OK, if it could light up.

00:43:50.127 --> 00:43:54.089
[Ami] After making a sample, I thought, this is, this is feasible.

00:43:54.089 --> 00:43:58.385
[Ami] And after realizing it was feasible, I got a second wind of energy. I said, OK, I'm going to start.

00:43:58.385 --> 00:44:03.724
[Ami] And at that time, I thought... because I'm actually a very frugal person. At my first job,

00:44:03.724 --> 00:44:07.394
[Ami] I saved up 100,000 Hong Kong dollars in a year. HKD.

00:44:07.394 --> 00:44:12.107
[Ami] So I thought, OK, I'll take a chunk of that money and make this thing.

00:44:12.107 --> 00:44:15.235
[Ami] So I wasn't... I wasn't actually very bold at the time.

00:44:15.235 --> 00:44:19.531
[Ami] I just said, OK, I'll take about one or two thousand yuan and test the waters.

00:44:19.573 --> 00:44:24.495
[Ami] Because I had just started working, one or two thousand yuan was still quite, quite a...

00:44:24.495 --> 00:44:26.622
[Ami] a large sum of money for me.

00:44:26.622 --> 00:44:32.461
[Ami] Then I went and did it. And after I started, I was actually not prepared at all. Because from product creation,

00:44:32.461 --> 00:44:37.925
[Ami] to needing a platform, you need a store, and then you have to list it, right?

00:44:37.925 --> 00:44:42.930
[Ami] Everything felt like I was being pushed into a situation I wasn't ready for.

00:44:42.930 --> 00:44:47.309
[Ami] So at the time, I gave this product to a KOL friend of mine.

00:44:47.309 --> 00:44:51.355
[Ami] And when he promoted it on his blog, it suddenly blew up.

00:44:51.355 --> 00:44:56.985
[Ami] But my whole preparation was maybe only at, like, 40 percent complete.

00:44:56.985 --> 00:50:01.990
[Ami] And as a result, with his push, I immediately rushed from 40 to, uh...

00:50:01.990 --> 00:50:03.659
[Ami] 100 percent progress.

00:50:03.659 --> 00:50:08.580
[Ami] So that brought me a very big... and to this day, it's a huge realization for me...

00:50:08.580 --> 00:50:10.833
[Ami] that "done" is better than "perfect."

00:50:10.833 --> 00:50:15.045
[Ami] You will never wait until a state where everything is perfectly ready.

00:50:15.045 --> 00:50:16.922
[Ami] So you just have to start doing it.

00:50:17.005 --> 00:50:20.926
[Ami] What was perhaps lucky for me at the time was that my friend gave me a push.

00:50:20.926 --> 00:50:26.890
[Ami] In a way, he brought me exposure, and on the other hand, he also pushed me to do this.

00:50:26.890 --> 00:50:31.770
[Ami] Uh, to quickly finish this task. Otherwise, I might have kept procrastinating...

00:50:31.770 --> 00:50:36.775
[Ami] and by the time I was done, someone else would have developed the product, and I would have lost the market share.

00:50:36.775 --> 00:50:41.739
[Ami] This was the first very important thing that starting a business taught me at that time:

00:50:41.739 --> 00:50:43.657
[Ami] "done" is better than "perfect."

00:50:43.782 --> 00:50:45.784
[Ray] Mmm, OK. You just mentioned being pushed into it.

00:50:45.784 --> 00:50:51.373
[Ray] So... was the reason because that KOL friend had already let the cat out of the bag,

00:50:51.373 --> 00:50:57.421
[Ray] and you had no way back? Or did you have a driving force from the beginning, thinking, "I need to get this thing out as soon as possible,

00:50:57.421 --> 00:51:00.841
[Ray] or someone else might get the idea first"?

00:51:00.841 --> 00:51:06.013
[Ami] Mmm, I think it was mainly the first one. I didn't actually think too much about it. I just thought my product was great,

00:51:06.013 --> 00:51:09.641
[Ami] and I wanted to get it out there. As for whether someone else would make it later,

00:51:09.641 --> 00:51:14.021
[Ami] I thought probably no one would think of such a weird idea like I did.

00:51:14.021 --> 00:51:18.609
[Ami] Then my friend gave me that push, which basically cut off my retreat. If I didn't launch,

00:51:18.609 --> 00:51:23.030
[Ami] I couldn't handle the traffic, and then my 100 units might not sell, right?

00:51:23.030 --> 00:51:28.327
[Ami] So I quickly listed it. And after listing it, maybe not long after,

00:51:28.327 --> 00:51:30.579
[Ami] the 100 units sold out in one day.

00:51:30.621 --> 00:51:34.249
[Ray] So that's a bit like what they call "build in public" now.

00:51:34.249 --> 00:51:37.211
[Ray] You announce it early on.

00:51:37.211 --> 00:51:40.714
[Ray] For example, if we announce today, say, "YikouXinFan,"

00:51:40.714 --> 00:51:45.219
[Ray] or that Ami's store has a new product, when maybe there's nothing yet,

00:51:45.219 --> 00:51:46.720
[Ray] once you announce it, you have to do it.

00:51:46.720 --> 00:51:52.392
[Ray] Actually, there are many approaches like this now. For example, I might sell a course or some product,

00:51:52.392 --> 00:51:56.522
[Ray] but actually, there's nothing yet, just an idea. You announce it, maybe even sell it first,

00:51:56.522 --> 00:51:59.733
[Ray] and that cuts off your retreat, forcing yourself to do it.

00:51:59.733 --> 00:52:03.070
[Ray] But basically, you'll still find that, actually, I...

00:52:03.070 --> 00:52:07.366
[Ray] I often say that people tell us we're just an example of survivorship bias.

00:52:07.366 --> 00:52:11.745
[Ray] They say that our so-called entrepreneurial success is just survivorship bias.

00:52:11.745 --> 00:52:15.874
[Ray] But I want to say, all of us, everyone watching this video, all of us together,

00:52:16.041 --> 00:52:18.210
[Ray] we are all survivors.

00:52:18.210 --> 00:52:22.923
[Ray] It's actually really hard to get to this point. Think about it, from being that little tadpole to finally becoming a living person,

00:52:22.923 --> 00:52:28.428
[Ray] and now watching this video, it's actually a very difficult thing. We are all...

00:52:28.428 --> 00:52:29.680
[Ray] we are all survivors.

00:52:29.721 --> 00:52:35.477
[Ray] So for many things you think might fail or whatever, if you really put it out there and do it,

00:52:35.477 --> 00:52:40.607
[Ray] if you look closely at your past so many years, you've always been that survivor.

00:52:40.607 --> 00:52:43.485
[Ray] So I think you have to have that confidence.

00:52:43.485 --> 00:52:46.989
[Ray] So back then, when you sold them, how much money did you make?

00:52:47.155 --> 00:52:51.910
[Ami] That was my first pot of gold. Not a lot, but maybe around... let me calculate,

00:52:51.910 --> 00:52:55.497
[Ami] a rough estimate would be over 10,000.

00:52:55.664 --> 00:52:59.084
[Ray] Over 10,000, and you invested one or two thousand.

00:52:59.126 --> 00:53:01.753
[Ami] No, I probably added more later, I added some more.

00:53:01.753 --> 00:53:06.884
[Ami] Anyway, with this product, in the first phase, it was about 10,000 to 20,000.

00:53:06.884 --> 00:53:07.885
[Ami] It was a small amount.

00:53:08.510 --> 00:53:12.931
[Ray] You said earlier you were very frugal. What does that mean?

00:53:13.056 --> 00:53:19.396
[Ami] Um, it's like... let's take an example. When I was working,

00:53:19.396 --> 00:53:25.193
[Ami] it's a bit embarrassing to say, but at the Chinese investment bank, the benefits were really good.

00:53:25.193 --> 00:53:29.156
[Ami] For lunch, you know in Hong Kong, eating a meal is quite expensive.

00:53:29.156 --> 00:53:35.412
[Ami] A char siu rice combo outside might cost 40 or 50 now, but our cafeteria was only 10 dollars.

00:53:35.412 --> 00:53:40.042
[Ami] It was an employee benefit, and you could get it to go. And there was dinner too, which you could also take away.

00:53:40.042 --> 00:53:45.464
[Ami] So every day, OK, I ate lunch at the company, and at night, I'd take it home.

00:53:45.464 --> 00:53:50.177
[Ami] And sometimes if I couldn't finish it, I might pack it up and eat it the next day.

00:53:50.177 --> 00:53:52.262
[Ami] It was a case of if I didn't have to spend money, I wouldn't.

00:53:52.262 --> 00:53:56.850
[Ami] Because I felt I would definitely, definitely have an opportunity where I needed to use money.

00:53:56.850 --> 00:54:01.772
[Ami] But as for what I would use it on, I didn't know. So I'd save it up first. That's how it was.

00:54:01.897 --> 00:54:06.818
[Ray] You know, I've seen that. I've seen that scenario. When I go to the Google cafeteria to eat,

00:54:07.110 --> 00:54:13.283
[Ray] I'd see people packing food to go, packing up salads and things like that, taking food...

00:54:13.283 --> 00:54:14.409
[Ray] to take home.

00:54:14.409 --> 00:54:19.623
[Ray] Speaking of being frugal, you said you invested one or two thousand. I just made a video recently

00:54:19.623 --> 00:54:22.918
[Ray] where I talked about "do not lose money." When we're starting a business,

00:54:22.918 --> 00:54:28.423
[Ray] you should try to make not losing money your number one goal.

00:54:28.423 --> 00:54:31.259
[Ray] So did you have that kind of consideration at the time?

00:54:31.718 --> 00:54:36.181
[Ami] At the time, I felt that in the very beginning, you have to take that risk.

00:54:36.181 --> 00:54:40.769
[Ami] It's not like now, where I might know, OK, I already have this audience base.

00:54:40.769 --> 00:54:45.983
[Ami] Or I have this store as a foundation. But in the beginning, I felt I could test it on a small scale,

00:54:45.983 --> 00:54:46.983
[Ami] but not too much.

00:54:46.983 --> 00:54:49.820
[Ami] I told myself, OK, if I lose 5,000, I'm done.

00:54:49.820 --> 00:54:54.116
[Ami] Because 5,000 for me back then could buy an iPad, it could buy something.

00:54:54.116 --> 00:54:58.954
[Ami] I would calculate, OK, with this money, if I don't invest it, what could I buy?

00:54:58.954 --> 00:55:03.834
[Ami] And if investing it was better than buying that thing, I would use it to test the waters a little.

00:55:03.834 --> 00:55:08.630
[Ami] But I think I would have a bottom line. OK, for me at the time, 5,000 yuan was my bottom line.

00:55:08.630 --> 00:55:12.217
[Ami] Any more than that, I probably wouldn't want to bear the loss.

00:55:12.259 --> 00:55:15.095
[Ray] Mmm. So in this process, with this product,

00:55:15.095 --> 00:55:21.018
[Ray] this was your first completed zero-to-one process, this closed loop of making money.

00:55:21.018 --> 00:55:23.228
[Ray] What was the most difficult part for you?

00:55:23.353 --> 00:55:27.607
[Ami] Hmm, I think the most difficult part was probably...

00:55:28.066 --> 00:55:31.611
[Ami] probably the stage from product design to production.

00:55:31.611 --> 00:55:35.991
[Ami] Of course, I don't think I can pick the most difficult one, because the whole zero-to-one process is very hard.

00:55:36.074 --> 00:55:36.450
[Ray] It's all hard.

00:55:36.491 --> 00:55:36.992
[Ami] Yes, it's all hard.

00:55:37.075 --> 00:55:41.872
[Ami] Because I feel that with everything, we might see, OK, this person has reached 100,

00:55:41.872 --> 00:55:45.625
[Ami] but in reality, the difficulty index of going from zero to one is

00:55:45.751 --> 00:55:48.420
[Ami] several times that of going from one to 99.

00:55:48.420 --> 00:55:51.089
[Ami] Including, for example, from the very beginning, OK,

00:55:51.089 --> 00:55:55.927
[Ami] you might make the product, that's one step. Then you have to list it, that's the second step.

00:55:55.927 --> 00:55:58.388
[Ami] The third step is you have to ship it, right?

00:55:58.388 --> 00:56:02.934
[Ami] For instance, I was in Hong Kong at the time, and my family was probably in Shanghai.

00:56:02.934 --> 00:56:07.981
[Ami] So at the time, I'd say, OK, for shipping, do I have to ask my parents for help?

00:56:08.148 --> 00:56:13.445
[Ami] So this entire... this entire process, after you've done it, you realize... Looking back on it now,

00:56:13.445 --> 00:56:19.534
[Ami] it's OK, it's a small thing. But when you were doing it at the time, fumbling your way from scratch, you were completely...

00:56:19.534 --> 00:56:25.248
[Ami] lost, like a headless fly. I feel every step was very difficult. But as long as you persevere,

00:56:25.248 --> 00:56:27.125
[Ami] then, OK, they are all small things.

00:56:27.209 --> 00:56:29.669
[Ray] So for you now, to develop another product,

00:56:29.669 --> 00:56:33.131
[Ray] this whole process, you feel it's... I wouldn't say easy, but you wouldn't...

00:56:33.215 --> 00:56:39.054
[Ami] Mmm, at least it's second nature. Once you've done it, I think it's just a matter of being a skilled worker.

00:56:39.054 --> 00:56:42.849
[Ami] But if you haven't done it, it's a very difficult thing.

00:56:42.974 --> 00:56:45.268
[Ray] Right. So besides the actual operations being difficult, more...

00:56:45.268 --> 00:56:49.689
[Ray] I think another big part is the cognitive aspect. From a mental...

00:56:49.689 --> 00:56:51.191
[Ray] that challenge is also huge.

00:56:51.191 --> 00:56:57.155
[Ray] Basically, although they're all small tasks, each step corresponds to a department in a large company, with many people doing it.

00:56:57.322 --> 00:57:02.994
[Ami] Right, right. So I think at the time, maybe I was very eager to get this product made.

00:57:02.994 --> 00:57:05.914
[Ami] I felt this thing was great, and I wanted to let others know.

00:57:05.914 --> 00:57:09.084
[Ami] I felt my idea was amazing, so I said, OK,

00:52:09.084 --> 00:52:11.628
[Ami] I felt it deserved to be seen by more people.

00:52:11.628 --> 00:52:17.008
[Ami] Of course, I think what happened next proved my idea. Back then, there was,

00:52:17.008 --> 00:52:22.764
[Ami] after I launched this product, a lot of people copied it. So, OK, I was actually a bit devastated at the time.

00:52:22.764 --> 00:52:26.977
[Ami] But then I thought about it. If people are copying me, it means the product is good. To what extent was it copied?

00:52:26.977 --> 00:52:32.190
[Ami] I saw someone on YouTube doing an unboxing of a counterfeit version, and I was like,

00:52:32.190 --> 00:52:35.026
[Ami] "Wow, it's being copied internationally now."

00:52:35.026 --> 00:52:37.112
[Ami] But I was probably still a little secretly pleased inside.

00:52:37.112 --> 00:52:41.199
[Ami] I felt that the product I created back then was truly very, very innovative.

00:52:41.199 --> 00:52:44.703
[Ami] So, even though the process at the time was very painful,

00:52:44.703 --> 00:52:48.164
[Ami] I feel the positive feedback it brought me was more than enough to offset that.

00:52:48.164 --> 00:52:51.418
[Ray] So you felt that after creating this first one, you could make a second, a third, a fourth.

00:52:51.585 --> 00:52:51.793
[Ami] Right.

00:52:51.835 --> 00:52:56.923
[Ray] So after you quit your last job, did you just focus wholeheartedly on this,

00:52:56.923 --> 00:52:58.675
[Ray] or did you have other plans?

00:52:58.800 --> 00:53:01.303
[Ami] After I quit my last job,

00:53:01.303 --> 00:53:05.557
[Ami] I mainly wanted to focus on my own creative merchandise business.

00:53:05.557 --> 00:53:10.270
[Ami] Because at that time, I felt that, how should I put it, the whole pace had become very stable.

00:53:10.270 --> 00:53:16.484
[Ami] From the first product until now, things had become quite automated, including things like shipping,

00:53:16.484 --> 00:53:20.113
[Ami] and product design. Once I became proficient in these operations,

00:53:20.113 --> 00:53:25.702
[Ami] all I had to do was constantly replicate this process, like a machine, just keep doing it and doing it,

00:53:25.702 --> 00:53:29.706
[Ami] and I felt I could reach a pretty ideal state.

00:53:29.706 --> 00:53:33.627
[Ami] So after I quit, I planned to focus on my creative merchandise business.

00:53:33.919 --> 00:53:38.381
[Ray] What about your second product and the ones after? Were there any other viral hits?

00:53:38.506 --> 00:53:44.262
[Ami] I knew at the time that after this format came out, the market response was quite good.

00:53:44.262 --> 00:53:48.975
[Ami] But there was a problem, which is that the product has a life cycle.

00:53:48.975 --> 00:53:54.814
[Ami] Calendars are something that, after the end of the year, or maybe you start selling in October,

00:53:54.814 --> 00:53:59.527
[Ami] you might only have a two-month life cycle. OK, so for this product, I remember clearly,

00:53:59.527 --> 00:54:04.074
[Ami] I probably started selling it in October, but by the end of the year, you'd find that no one was buying it.

00:54:04.074 --> 00:54:09.287
[Ami] Also, at that point, I could afford some inventory losses.

00:54:09.287 --> 00:54:14.167
[Ami] For example, maybe out of 500 units sold, there were about 50 left. OK, I'll just write them off,

00:54:14.167 --> 00:54:14.251
[Ami] right?

00:54:14.292 --> 00:54:18.088
[Ami] But if I didn't create new products, this brand,

00:54:18.088 --> 00:54:24.094
[Ami] or this little side hustle of yours, wouldn't be sustainable. Once you sell out of this item, OK, it becomes stagnant.

00:54:24.094 --> 00:54:24.886
[Ami] If you don't create anything new.

00:54:24.928 --> 00:54:29.432
[Ami] So later, I made a second product. And this second product had a similar format,

00:54:29.432 --> 00:54:32.310
[Ami] because I thought, OK, if the first one was a hit, the second one will be too.

00:54:32.352 --> 00:54:37.315
[Ami] And as it turned out, it was indeed a hit. It was a more evergreen product.

00:54:37.315 --> 00:54:43.405
[Ami] It was a memo board that you could use every day, so it wasn't limited by the year or...

00:54:43.405 --> 00:54:44.406
[Ray] Do you have it here?

00:54:44.698 --> 00:54:47.158
[Ami] I didn't bring it today because we're not producing it anymore.

00:54:47.158 --> 00:54:47.617
[Ray] It's out of production.

00:54:47.659 --> 00:54:49.369
[Ami] Because it was copied too much.

00:54:49.369 --> 00:54:53.665
[Ami] And after that product came out, it was also very popular.

00:54:53.665 --> 00:54:59.379
[Ami] But, you know, you can't just keep making acrylic boards and light-up calendars forever.

00:54:59.379 --> 00:55:04.676
[Ami] So later I started to go back to my original passion, because I originally wanted to make journaling products.

00:55:04.676 --> 00:55:09.639
[Ami] At that time, I thought, OK, I want to create content related to creative stationery.

00:55:09.639 --> 00:55:15.311
[Ami] This is something I personally like, and I feel it's an area where I have an advantage.

00:55:15.311 --> 00:55:19.107
[Ami] So, at that time, because I was active in the journaling community,

00:55:19.107 --> 00:55:23.945
[Ami] I knew that in our stationery circle, there's this thing called stamps.

00:55:23.945 --> 00:55:24.738
[Ami] (Laughs)

00:55:24.779 --> 00:55:30.535
[Ami] I brought a sample. This is something you can use for life logging.

00:55:30.535 --> 00:55:33.288
[Ami] For many stamps, you just press it down, and OK, that's it.

00:55:33.288 --> 00:55:36.082
[Ami] But with this one, you can, for example, fill in things yourself.

00:55:36.082 --> 00:55:39.252
[Ami] Let's say this is a reading tracker. At our book club meetings,

00:55:39.252 --> 00:55:44.549
[Ami] you can, for instance, track what percentage of a book you've read. I think there's a certain ritual in recording things like this.

00:55:44.591 --> 00:55:49.804
[Ami] Or things like this, expenses and income. I love this one because I really enjoy ticking off the income.

00:55:49.804 --> 00:55:54.768
[Ami] It shows I have money coming in. Or you can fill in your expenses. Things like that.

00:55:54.768 --> 00:55:59.856
[Ami] Um, at the time, not many people in China were making these. And the category of stamps itself,

00:55:59.856 --> 00:56:01.566
[Ami] not many people were doing it.

00:56:01.566 --> 00:56:06.154
[Ami] Back then, I leveraged what I think was my unfair advantage.

00:56:06.154 --> 00:56:11.117
[Ami] Because I was in Hong Kong, you could see a lot of Japanese creators.

00:56:11.117 --> 00:56:17.373
[Ami] Or you could see the works of many creators from other countries online. And at that time, I discovered that, hey,

00:56:17.373 --> 00:56:21.628
[Ami] in Japan, they have this stamp brand called Sankodo.

00:56:21.628 --> 00:56:24.964
[Ami] A lot of their products are for you to record things yourself,

00:56:24.964 --> 00:56:30.387
[Ami] not just stamps that you press down once. I found that very interesting, but no one in mainland China was making them.

00:56:30.387 --> 00:56:35.767
[Ami] And at the same time, many people who acted as purchasing agents for these Japanese stamps sold them at very high prices.

00:56:35.767 --> 00:56:38.770
[Ami] Plus, even if they were expensive, you couldn't always get them.

00:56:38.812 --> 00:56:43.566
[Ami] So I thought, "Hey, why don't I fill this market gap?"

00:56:43.566 --> 00:56:47.403
[Ami] So this is how I think I used my small unfair advantage.

00:56:47.403 --> 00:56:50.073
[Ami] I felt, OK, I understand the stationery market,

00:56:50.073 --> 00:56:53.660
[Ami] and at the same time, I had access to the foreign stationery market.

00:56:53.660 --> 00:56:56.996
[Ami] So I just filled the gap that was missing in the domestic market.

00:56:56.996 --> 00:57:02.043
[Ami] So at that time, I made a batch like this. And after the first batch came out,

00:57:02.043 --> 00:57:07.173
[Ami] I think... I actually had no idea it would become so popular, even to this day.

00:57:07.173 --> 00:57:11.261
[Ami] This product still sells very well. And of course, it has been copied by many people.

00:57:11.261 --> 00:57:17.350
[Ami] This indirectly proves that there is a lot of demand for it in the market.

00:57:17.350 --> 00:57:19.144
[Ami] It's a reflection of that.

00:57:19.144 --> 00:57:23.314
[Ami] So I feel that, OK, if during the entrepreneurial process, you can

00:57:23.314 --> 00:57:28.027
[Ami] get access to some information that others don't know, or in this internet age,

00:57:28.027 --> 00:57:33.074
[Ami] information is your capital. If you can make good use of your unfair advantage

00:57:33.074 --> 00:57:38.037
[Ami] to access more information and then apply it to fill a market gap,

00:57:38.037 --> 00:57:43.042
[Ami] I think you can achieve the result of making a bit of money.

00:57:43.084 --> 00:57:48.256
[Ray] Right. You said this was extremely popular. Compared to the first product, how popular was it?

00:57:48.465 --> 00:57:51.843
[Ami] Um, how popular was this one?

00:57:51.843 --> 00:57:57.724
[Ami] The first one I think was cyclical, or its format was very singular.

00:57:57.724 --> 00:58:02.937
[Ami] But for this one, as long as you change the design on it, or if people have a need for it...

00:58:02.937 --> 00:58:08.401
[Ami] Uh, for example, for meals, we made small stamps for eating, showering,

00:58:08.401 --> 00:58:12.739
[Ami] drinking water... anything in your life. We have a series called "Life Logging."

00:58:12.739 --> 00:58:17.076
[Ami] Anything in your life that's worth recording can be made into a product like this.

00:58:17.076 --> 00:58:21.164
[Ami] So its applicability or consumer base is much broader.

00:58:21.164 --> 00:58:26.503
[Ami] So its overall... I've sold about four...

00:58:26.503 --> 00:58:31.341
[Ami] four series so far, and the sales for each series are over a thousand units.

00:58:31.382 --> 00:58:31.424
[Ray] Mmm.

00:58:31.507 --> 00:58:34.469
[Ami] Right, sales are over a thousand. Something like that.

00:58:34.510 --> 00:58:35.929
[Ray] One thousand in total for one of them?

00:58:35.970 --> 00:58:37.430
[Ami] No, no, just for a single one.

00:58:37.514 --> 00:58:38.765
[Ray] For a single one, mm-hmm.

00:58:38.765 --> 00:58:39.390
[Ami] Right, right, right.

00:58:39.432 --> 00:58:45.438
[Ami] So including later on, I saw in some stationery stores, they might have just copied this format,

00:58:45.438 --> 00:58:51.027
[Ami] or many similar products emerged later. I think this indirectly reflects that this format

00:58:51.027 --> 00:58:56.032
[Ami] has a lot of market... market potential.

00:58:56.074 --> 00:59:01.454
[Ray] Mmm. You just mentioned this "unfair advantage." How do you define that concept?

00:59:01.454 --> 00:59:03.748
[Ray] Or where did you first hear about it?

00:59:04.040 --> 00:59:09.045
[Ami] Uh, at the time, I had read a book. I didn't know at first, OK, that this product was an example of...

00:59:09.045 --> 00:59:15.009
[Ami] proof of my unfair advantage. It wasn't until later that I read a book, and its title was "The Unfair Advantage."

00:59:15.009 --> 00:59:18.263
[Ami] In it, there's a... a formula.

00:59:18.263 --> 00:59:23.559
[Ami] You can look at various aspects like your family background, your skills, and all sorts of things,

00:59:23.559 --> 00:59:28.815
[Ami] and analyze yourself to see if you have anything different from or better than others.

00:59:28.815 --> 00:59:32.485
[Ami] Like the story I just told about my first Russian boss.

00:59:32.485 --> 00:59:35.822
[Ami] She discovered that her skill was perhaps in communication,

00:59:35.822 --> 00:59:39.867
[Ami] or that she was very eloquent. OK, she found this unfair advantage and magnified it.

00:59:39.867 --> 00:59:44.872
[Ami] For me, my unfair advantage might be that I understand the domestic market,

00:59:44.872 --> 00:59:50.461
[Ami] and at the same time, I have access to more foreign information, or non-domestic information.

00:59:50.461 --> 00:59:53.923
[Ami] Then I can, so to speak, apply it.

00:59:53.923 --> 00:59:59.887
[Ami] I can apply what I see to the domestic market and do things that no one in the domestic market is doing.

00:59:59.887 --> 01:00:03.016
[Ami] I think that's how I can leverage this information asymmetry

01:00:03.016 --> 01:00:06.519
[Ami] to achieve the effect of amplifying my advantage.

01:00:06.728 --> 01:00:12.191
[Ray] You mentioned earlier that, uh, you are in this creative merchandise circle,

01:00:12.191 --> 01:00:15.570
[Ray] meaning you have a genuine passion for creative merchandise.

01:00:15.570 --> 01:00:17.530
[Ray] Why do I bring this up?

01:00:17.530 --> 01:00:22.994
[Ray] Because many people will say, "I want to get into e-commerce, but I don't know how to select products."

01:00:22.994 --> 01:00:25.913
[Ray] But I see in you another unfair advantage,

01:00:25.913 --> 01:00:30.543
[Ray] which is that you actually have a small community, a little circle, that you're passionate about.

01:00:30.543 --> 01:00:35.089
[Ray] It's like the book we're all reading this month. I know you've read it before, too.

01:00:35.089 --> 01:00:39.802
[Ray] We talked about that book the first time we met—"Small is Beautiful."

01:00:39.802 --> 01:00:40.011
[Ray] Right.

01:00:40.053 --> 01:00:43.973
[Ray] The minimalist entrepreneur, yeah. It mentions

01:00:43.973 --> 01:00:45.808
[Ray] community, the so-called community.

01:00:45.808 --> 01:00:51.939
[Ray] It's about which circle you belong to. Only within that circle can you know what people like,

01:00:51.939 --> 01:00:53.107
[Ray] what people are thinking about.

01:00:53.149 --> 01:00:58.571
[Ray] Or, in other words, it's about fulfilling your own needs.

01:00:58.780 --> 01:01:03.868
[Ray] So, up to now, I want to ask you, with your current creative merchandise business,

01:01:03.868 --> 01:01:08.706
[Ray] in that circle... it's called "shouzhang" (journaling). For someone like me,

01:01:08.831 --> 01:01:12.919
[Ray] I actually didn't even know what "shouzhang" was. What's it for? Recording finances? How much I spent today?

01:01:12.919 --> 01:01:17.507
[Ray] I always thought "shouzhang" was for keeping accounts, that kind of thing. Later I learned, oh,

01:01:17.507 --> 01:01:20.593
[Ray] so that's what it is. To be honest, I still might not fully get it.

01:01:20.677 --> 01:01:26.015
[Ray] So you see, for me to create a product like that would be very difficult. I'd be making it just for the sake of making it.

01:01:26.015 --> 01:01:26.933
[Ray] So I want to ask you now,

01:01:26.974 --> 01:01:32.271
[Ray] are you still in that community? Like, are you still using your own journal every day?

01:01:32.438 --> 01:01:33.731
[Ami] Yes, I do.

01:01:34.107 --> 01:01:35.858
[Ray] Do you... do you have your journal with you or anything?

01:01:35.983 --> 01:01:37.985
[Ami] Uh, no, I didn't bring it in.

01:01:37.985 --> 01:01:39.737
[Ami] Right, because I think what's inside is a bit...

01:01:39.737 --> 01:01:41.239
[Ray] A bit personal.

01:01:41.239 --> 01:01:41.739
[Ray] Mmm.

01:01:41.781 --> 01:01:48.079
[Ami] Right. But I think this also counts as an advantage. I genuinely love this stuff.

01:01:48.079 --> 01:01:52.917
[Ami] I think that for many... if I compare it to some big companies, they might,

01:01:52.917 --> 01:01:58.381
[Ami] in the end, be doing it for profit. But for me, it might be, OK, I really want to use this thing myself,

01:01:58.381 --> 01:02:03.928
[Ami] so I produce it. And I also know, OK, if I need this, then other people will need it too.

01:02:03.928 --> 01:02:08.433
[Ami] And because I understand this field, I know, OK, this community of mine,

01:02:08.433 --> 01:02:11.769
[Ami] what kind of things they need. Then if I produce it,

01:02:11.769 --> 01:02:16.774
[Ami] it achieves a good cycle of "you need it, I supply it."

01:02:16.774 --> 01:02:22.613
[Ami] But at the same time, I myself... like even now, every time I create a product, I still get very, very excited.

01:02:22.613 --> 01:02:26.742
[Ami] I'll say, "Oh!" From just a design draft,

01:02:26.742 --> 01:02:30.246
[Ami] when I actually get my hands on the physical object, I'll think, "Wow, so this is..."

01:02:30.246 --> 01:02:36.252
[Ami] "This is what it's like as a physical product." I genuinely want to take it out and use it, and I want to tell the whole world

01:02:36.252 --> 01:02:40.465
[Ami] that I made this thing. So I think this passion,

01:02:40.465 --> 01:02:44.760
[Ami] this enthusiasm, is also something you really need when you're starting a business.

01:02:44.760 --> 01:02:49.307
[Ami] You can't let this venture extinguish this... this hobby of yours. Right.

01:02:49.348 --> 01:02:52.226
[Ray] So it still comes down to truly loving something.

01:02:52.226 --> 01:02:52.727
[Ami] Right.

01:02:52.768 --> 01:02:57.732
[Ray] So rather than saying, "I want to do e-commerce," or "I want to do any so-called startup,"

01:02:57.732 --> 01:03:00.109
[Ray] thinking about what kind of product or service I should create,

01:03:00.109 --> 01:03:03.780
[Ray] it's better to make yourself a richer, more interesting person.

01:03:03.780 --> 01:03:06.449
[Ray] You have things you genuinely love,

01:03:06.449 --> 01:03:11.537
[Ray] and that's where you get this kind of inspiration. OK, so for "unfair advantage," you mentioned the information gap,

01:03:11.537 --> 01:03:16.209
[Ray] and you said you can understand foreign things. I know that includes English,

01:03:16.209 --> 01:03:18.252
[Ray] and now maybe also Japanese.

01:03:18.252 --> 01:03:19.170
[Ami] A little bit.

01:03:19.253 --> 01:03:24.258
[Ray] A little Japanese. You also mentioned earlier, I think I heard you say your English is quite good.

01:03:24.258 --> 01:03:26.385
[Ray] Your boss could see you had that ability.

01:03:26.385 --> 01:03:27.553
[Ray] So what's the story with your English?

01:03:27.553 --> 01:03:30.223
[Ray] You weren't an English major originally.

01:03:30.348 --> 01:03:33.184
[Ami] Right. It's because my... I don't know if you guys know,

01:03:33.184 --> 01:03:37.855
[Ami] but my undergraduate degree was from Beijing Foreign Studies University.

01:03:37.855 --> 01:03:43.402
[Ami] First of all, our university is very strict about English. It's like, OK, if you can't speak English well,

01:03:43.402 --> 01:03:45.655
[Ami] you don't deserve to be at this school.

01:03:45.655 --> 01:03:51.452
[Ami] Secondly, back then, I felt... you know, as a journalism student,

01:03:51.452 --> 01:03:54.956
[Ami] you're not just limited to domestic crises.

01:03:54.956 --> 01:03:59.293
[Ami] You want to understand more about what the outside world is like.

01:03:59.293 --> 01:04:03.381
[Ami] And including the information you might see,

01:04:03.381 --> 01:04:08.511
[Ami] or, OK, you... as you said earlier, once you've tasted freedom,

01:04:08.594 --> 01:04:12.473
[Ami] once you've seen a wider world, you develop a greater thirst for knowledge.

01:04:12.473 --> 01:04:18.229
[Ami] So at the time, I felt that, OK, learning English well... there's the old saying, "Master math, physics, and chemistry, and you can take on the world,"

01:04:18.229 --> 01:04:24.402
[Ami] but I think learning English well might be the skill that truly lets you open your eyes to the world.

01:04:24.402 --> 01:04:25.611
[Ami] A skill like that.

01:04:25.736 --> 01:04:29.824
[Ray] Right, I've always thought so too. I once posted something very extreme.

01:04:29.824 --> 01:04:33.286
[Ray] I felt that among other things, the one thing you can learn in university

01:04:33.286 --> 01:04:38.165
[Ray] that will be applicable for your entire life is probably English.

01:04:38.165 --> 01:04:40.084
[Ray] Something that lasts a lifetime.

01:04:40.084 --> 01:04:44.255
[Ray] It's a bit extreme, of course, but I think it highlights the importance of English.

01:04:44.297 --> 01:04:44.964
[Ray] OK.

01:04:45.006 --> 01:04:51.345
[Ray] Listening to this, it sounds like your entrepreneurial journey, after you quit your job, has been very smooth. Is that the case?

01:04:51.345 --> 01:04:56.684
[Ami] Um, actually, to many people, when you look at a successful person, you'll always think their journey was smooth.

01:04:56.684 --> 01:05:02.607
[Ami] But in reality, I think from the very beginning until now, there have always been small setbacks.

01:05:02.607 --> 01:05:08.029
[Ami] It's just that for people like us who are content creators, you're likely... more often than not, you're performing, you know?

01:05:08.029 --> 01:05:10.531
[Ami] You have to perform a very successful image.

01:05:10.531 --> 01:05:15.077
[Ami] But in reality, in the very beginning, after creating those two so-called "hit products,"

01:05:15.077 --> 01:05:17.955
[Ami] I actually hit my first slump.

01:05:17.955 --> 01:05:22.710
[Ami] And that's also why I ended up getting that third job later on.

01:05:22.710 --> 01:05:26.922
[Ami] After I made those two products, I became inexplicably confident.

01:05:26.922 --> 01:05:32.261
[Ami] I just thought, OK, if I launch a third product, it will definitely be as big a hit as these two.

01:05:32.261 --> 01:05:37.975
[Ami] Back then, in the cultural and creative products circle, what was popular was that you'd draw a design,

01:05:37.975 --> 01:05:39.185
[Ami] I brought it with me today. It's this one.

01:05:39.185 --> 01:05:45.274
[Ami] This is the series of patterns I made for my third product. OK, I actually think this design is very... a

01:05:45.274 --> 01:05:45.816
[Ami] bit childish.

01:05:45.858 --> 01:05:49.737
[Ami] At least in my opinion. But back then, I just made this kind of design.

01:05:49.779 --> 01:05:54.450
[Ami] OK, I thought, I'll print it on a mug, and then on a phone case.

01:05:54.450 --> 01:05:56.369
[Ami] That's what everyone was doing back then.

01:05:56.369 --> 01:05:58.954
[Ami] So I thought, OK, I'll follow the same path.

01:05:58.954 --> 01:06:03.834
[Ami] Also, you can't just make low-priced products, so I thought at the time,

01:06:03.876 --> 01:06:08.047
[Ami] OK, everyone loves using iPads, so I made this iPad case.

01:06:08.047 --> 01:06:12.718
[Ami] And when I made this iPad case, I thought, "Wow, this is amazing."

01:06:12.718 --> 01:06:16.597
[Ami] It's like your own child; you never think they're ugly. I thought, "Oh, it's so beautiful."

01:06:16.597 --> 01:06:22.603
[Ami] The colors and everything were exactly as I had imagined, so I thought this thing would be a hit.

01:06:22.603 --> 01:06:28.234
[Ami] So I was full of confidence. For typical cultural and creative products, the minimum order quantity might be quite small.

01:06:28.234 --> 01:06:33.239
[Ami] For example, if you print this design on a phone case, the minimum order quantity might be as low as thirty.

01:06:33.239 --> 01:06:35.241
[Ami] So the financial pressure is very low.

01:06:35.241 --> 01:06:39.912
[Ami] But for a product like this, the minimum order quantity is at least five hundred.

01:06:39.912 --> 01:06:44.041
[Ami] Then I thought, OK, since I've already made an iPad case, I should make a laptop case too.

01:06:44.041 --> 01:06:48.170
[Ami] So I also made a larger size. Just like that, I had a thousand of them made.

01:06:48.170 --> 01:06:52.341
[Ami] At the time, I thought, "A thousand units? That's easy. I sold five hundred of the other one,"

01:06:52.341 --> 01:06:56.429
[Ami] "and that only took me about a week." So I was full of confidence.

01:06:56.429 --> 01:07:01.934
[Ami] I even hyped it up a bit on my social media beforehand, thinking, "Yeah, this should sell well."

01:07:02.018 --> 01:07:07.690
[Ami] But after it was listed, only a few loyal fans bought one. I think I sold maybe a dozen.

01:07:07.690 --> 01:07:12.862
[Ami] Out of a thousand units, I'd only sold about a dozen. That was a huge, huge blow to me at the time.

01:07:12.862 --> 01:07:16.991
[Ami] Before the launch, scheduled for 6 p.m., I sat in front of my computer.

01:07:16.991 --> 01:07:20.161
[Ami] I was sure the sales numbers would just shoot up.

01:07:20.161 --> 01:07:24.498
[Ami] But as it turned out, only one sold in the first hour, and maybe two or three in the next.

01:07:24.498 --> 01:07:29.628
[Ami] At that point, I actually thought, "Oh no, maybe I didn't do the marketing right,"

01:07:29.628 --> 01:07:31.338
[Ami] "or I didn't promote it well enough."

01:07:31.338 --> 01:07:35.551
[Ami] So afterwards, I did more things, like filming a lot of videos,

01:07:35.968 --> 01:07:41.474
[Ami] and showing how to use it. It felt like I was holding it up saying, "This is great, just buy it!"

01:07:41.474 --> 01:07:43.184
[Ami] That was the feeling, you know?

01:07:43.184 --> 01:07:48.147
[Ami] But even after about a month, the sales still hadn't picked up.

01:07:48.147 --> 01:07:52.485
[Ami] So eventually I thought, "Forget it, I should just stick to my day job," and I kind of gave up.

01:07:52.485 --> 01:07:58.449
[Ami] I kind of gave up on the idea of turning my side hustle into my main job, so I just waited.

01:07:58.449 --> 01:08:03.329
[Ami] I figured I'd just let them sit there. My house was piled up with a thousand laptop cases at the time.

01:08:03.329 --> 01:08:09.001
[Ami] My mom almost scolded me to death. She'd ask, "When are these going to sell?" We were selling maybe one or two a day.

01:08:09.001 --> 01:08:14.507
[Ami] Just one or two a day. But then, after about half a year, around the six-month mark,

01:08:14.507 --> 01:08:19.970
[Ami] suddenly, you'd notice, hey, out of a thousand, there were only about eight hundred left... which wasn't a huge amount,

01:08:19.970 --> 01:08:25.976
[Ami] but the sales started to grow exponentially. I found that very interesting.

01:08:26.018 --> 01:08:31.565
[Ami] Later, I talked to a friend in e-commerce, and he gave me some insight.

01:08:31.565 --> 01:08:37.113
[Ami] He said it was probably because I had a new shop. If you don't have enough sales volume,

01:08:37.113 --> 01:08:39.365
[Ami] the platform won't give you any exposure.

01:08:39.365 --> 01:08:43.494
[Ami] So once my product started selling, say, after I sold at least two hundred units,

01:08:43.494 --> 01:08:49.125
[Ami] the platform began to see my shop as having potential. It was like, "OK, let's promote you a bit."

01:08:49.125 --> 01:08:53.379
[Ami] Back then, there weren't very many shops making this kind of product.

01:08:53.379 --> 01:08:59.260
[Ami] Most of the designs were cute and cartoonish, and then they'd see mine and think it was quite different.

01:08:59.260 --> 01:09:02.096
[Ami] That's when customers started coming in.

01:09:02.096 --> 01:09:06.600
[Ami] After that, about... about six months later—I remember it very clearly—

01:09:06.600 --> 01:09:09.478
[Ami] after six months, I started to turn a profit.

01:09:09.478 --> 01:09:14.650
[Ami] The money I had invested was suddenly all recouped.

01:09:14.650 --> 01:09:20.614
[Ami] After I recouped it, I felt that was my take-off point. Because once you've recouped your investment,

01:09:20.614 --> 01:09:25.369
[Ami] all the money you earn is no longer just profit, but your... your...

01:09:25.369 --> 01:09:26.203
[Ray] It's no longer just covering costs.

01:09:26.245 --> 01:09:27.746
[Ami] It's no longer covering costs; it's pure profit.

01:09:27.746 --> 01:09:32.918
[Ami] For example, let's say your cost was about one yuan, and you sold it for five yuan.

01:09:32.918 --> 01:09:37.298
[Ami] You'd only make four yuan in profit. But after you break even, you're earning the full five yuan.

01:09:37.298 --> 01:09:43.137
[Ami] After that point, I found that the money started to just... ka-ching.

01:09:43.137 --> 01:09:45.055
[Ami] That "ka-ching" feeling started to kick in.

01:09:45.055 --> 01:09:49.894
[Ami] So, OK, after that point, I waited for about another three or four months.

01:09:49.894 --> 01:09:54.773
[Ami] I felt that its growth had stabilized and I had made a bit of money, so I started getting restless again.

01:09:54.773 --> 01:09:58.194
[Ami] I said, "I'm going to make my fourth, my fourth product series."

01:09:58.194 --> 01:10:03.616
[Ami] Then after about two or three months, I started making these stamps.

01:10:03.616 --> 01:10:06.160
[Ami] And that led to another period of explosive growth.

01:10:06.160 --> 01:10:10.998
[Ami] So I really think that if I hadn't persevered through that dark period,

01:10:10.998 --> 01:10:16.462
[Ami] or if I'd just liquidated the stock thinking it wouldn't sell, I probably wouldn't be sitting here

01:10:16.462 --> 01:10:18.380
[Ami] talking to you about this.

01:10:18.422 --> 01:10:23.677
[Ami] So I think the third major lesson that entrepreneurship taught me

01:10:23.677 --> 01:10:26.472
[Ami] is that you have to be patient with success.

01:10:26.472 --> 01:10:31.310
[Ami] I also came across a very interesting saying later on. It says that progress...

01:10:31.310 --> 01:10:37.274
[Ami] isn't linear, but rather a spiral staircase. Many of us might think progress is linear, but it's not.

01:10:37.274 --> 01:10:41.904
[Ami] You might think you're not making much progress, but that might be because of your perspective.

01:10:41.904 --> 01:10:46.492
[Ami] But if you look at it differently, you're actually in the process of spiraling upwards.

01:10:46.492 --> 01:10:50.579
[Ami] You might just have to wait, and if you wait long enough,

01:10:50.579 --> 01:10:56.126
[Ami] you'll reach what we call the compounding point, or the tipping point.

01:10:56.293 --> 01:11:01.548
[Ray] Hmm, from what you've said about your products, it sounds like even though there were setbacks,

01:11:01.548 --> 01:11:03.300
[Ray] they all eventually sold out and made you money.

01:11:03.300 --> 01:11:08.722
[Ray] Have you never had a product that was a complete failure, one that you just gave up on?

01:11:08.889 --> 01:11:10.099
[Ami] Oh, yes, of course.

01:11:10.099 --> 01:11:14.645
[Ami] Actually, this was just a few months ago.

01:11:14.645 --> 01:11:16.522
[Ami] What was the product?

01:11:16.522 --> 01:11:17.982
[Ami] It was a planner.

01:11:17.982 --> 01:11:24.029
[Ami] At that point... I rarely make high-priced items, because my goal is to sell in high volume.

01:11:24.029 --> 01:11:29.618
[Ami] I'm not the type to make handmade items to sell; that's too time-consuming.

01:11:29.618 --> 01:11:35.541
[Ami] But at that time, I wanted to make a planner, and when we got to the material selection stage,

01:11:35.541 --> 01:11:41.630
[Ami] many of my fans, including customers who had bought our planners before,

01:11:41.630 --> 01:11:46.218
[Ami] saw the design and suggested that I make a genuine leather version.

01:11:46.218 --> 01:11:51.807
[Ami] We usually make these planners with what we call PU leather, or artificial leather.

01:11:51.807 --> 01:11:54.685
[Ami] The material is cheaper,

01:11:54.685 --> 01:11:58.063
[Ami] so I could afford to produce a batch of five hundred.

01:11:58.063 --> 01:12:00.315
[Ami] But the fans said, "This...

01:12:00.315 --> 01:12:05.154
[Ami] The shape is great." They said, "Can you make it in genuine leather?" And I thought,

01:12:05.154 --> 01:12:10.451
[Ami] I actually wanted to make one for myself. When I was designing it, I only intended it for personal use, but then I realized, hey,

01:12:10.451 --> 01:12:12.786
[Ami] there's a demand for this, so maybe I should make them?

01:12:12.828 --> 01:12:17.458
[Ami] But a big challenge for me was the high cost.

01:12:17.458 --> 01:12:23.130
[Ami] The production cost for a single planner was around one to two hundred yuan,

01:12:23.130 --> 01:12:26.967
[Ami] depending on the craftsmanship. And I couldn't set the selling price too high,

01:12:26.967 --> 01:12:30.637
[Ami] because my fan base couldn't afford such a high price.

01:12:30.637 --> 01:12:36.518
[Ami] So I decided, OK, I'll do a small batch. I found someone to make them by hand, and I had about fifty made.

01:12:36.518 --> 01:12:42.733
[Ami] Of course, the total cost wasn't extremely high, but for me, for just fifty units,

01:12:42.733 --> 01:12:48.030
[Ami] to produce such a small quantity for that amount of money was still very, very expensive.

01:12:48.030 --> 01:12:54.286
[Ami] After they were made, I started selling them. I thought, "This is what people wanted, right?"

01:12:54.286 --> 01:12:57.206
[Ami] But after I started selling, I found that people weren't buying them, you know?

01:12:57.206 --> 01:13:01.710
[Ami] Because for a high-priced product like this, it's different from the things I used to sell.

01:13:01.710 --> 01:13:05.714
[Ami] If a customer had a problem, I could just give them a refund or an exchange.

01:13:05.714 --> 01:13:11.345
[Ami] But for a high-priced product like this, we didn't support returns or exchanges, because if it's returned,

01:13:11.345 --> 01:13:14.181
[Ami] a genuine leather item can't be resold.

01:13:14.223 --> 01:13:20.312
[Ami] And secondly, before buying, you had to... you had to...

01:13:20.312 --> 01:13:22.815
[Ami] you had to understand that it couldn't be returned or exchanged.

01:13:22.815 --> 01:13:27.319
[Ami] So, after some fans bought it and received it,

01:13:27.319 --> 01:13:31.740
[Ami] they felt the product wasn't good. And after deciding they didn't like it,

01:13:31.740 --> 01:13:37.079
[Ami] they started posting their thoughts about it on social media.

01:13:37.079 --> 01:13:38.789
[Ami] And we completely understood.

01:13:38.789 --> 01:13:44.628
[Ami] I said, "OK, if you don't like it, we can give you a refund," and we even let them keep the item.

01:13:44.628 --> 01:13:50.759
[Ami] But that product had so many after-sales issues. I started to reflect and thought,

01:13:50.759 --> 01:13:56.598
[Ami] "Did I really want to make this product, or did I just make it because everyone wanted me to?"

01:13:56.849 --> 01:14:02.604
[Ami] Then I... I was reading a book at the time, and coincidentally,

01:14:02.604 --> 01:14:03.939
[Ami] you had just posted a video

01:14:03.939 --> 01:14:08.610
[Ami] about teaching people to live more selfishly.

01:14:08.610 --> 01:14:12.906
[Ami] So at that moment, I wondered if, when I made that decision,

01:14:12.906 --> 01:14:17.494
[Ami] I had acted from a purely generous standpoint, thinking, "You need this, so I'll make it,"

01:14:17.494 --> 01:14:22.416
[Ami] rather than from my own desire, thinking, "OK, I think I need this,

01:14:22.416 --> 01:14:26.712
[Ami] and I want to make this product." So it completely went against my original intention.

01:14:26.753 --> 01:14:30.757
[Ami] So that was a pretty big blow to me recently.

01:14:30.757 --> 01:14:32.342
[Ami] So I decided, OK,

01:14:32.342 --> 01:14:38.348
[Ami] from now on, in all my product development decisions, I need to be more selfish

01:14:38.348 --> 01:14:43.687
[Ami] and not let the market make decisions for me. Instead, after I make a decision,

01:14:43.687 --> 01:14:47.649
[Ami] I'll adjust based on market feedback. That should be the logic.

01:14:47.649 --> 01:14:53.614
[Ami] So this has been a pretty big blow to me recently in my entrepreneurial journey, yes.

01:14:53.614 --> 01:14:59.995
[Ray] So, in your entrepreneurial journey, the other half is your social media presence. Let's talk about how that developed.

01:15:00.454 --> 01:15:04.666
[Ami] Mmm, social media... I feel like it's the fertile ground that nurtured everything.

01:15:04.666 --> 01:15:10.088
[Ami] It's the cornerstone of everything. Without my social media presence,

01:15:10.088 --> 01:15:12.257
[Ami] I probably wouldn't have this cultural and creative business.

01:15:12.257 --> 01:15:17.512
[Ami] I actually started way back on the domestic platform, Weibo.

01:15:17.512 --> 01:15:21.600
[Ami] I can tell you the first reason I was able to grow that account.

01:15:21.892 --> 01:15:27.231
[Ami] I feel like I did something incredible: I posted an update every day for 365 days.

01:15:27.314 --> 01:15:28.315
[Ray] What were the updates?

01:15:28.357 --> 01:15:29.900
[Ami] My planner entries.

01:15:29.900 --> 01:15:33.237
[Ami] Why did I decide to do that?

01:15:33.237 --> 01:15:38.951
[Ami] Well, it was my last year of university, and I had a lot of free time, so I thought...

01:15:38.951 --> 01:15:43.789
[Ami] And the planner I had at the time was what we call a "page-a-day" planner.

01:15:43.789 --> 01:15:45.249
[Ami] You write one page every day.

01:15:45.249 --> 01:15:48.585
[Ami] So I thought, hey, now that I have this planner,

01:15:48.585 --> 01:15:51.296
[Ami] maybe I could post an update every single day?

01:15:51.380 --> 01:15:53.924
[Ami] Just to create that kind of content.

01:15:53.924 --> 01:15:58.428
[Ami] I thought it would be a pretty interesting project to do for a whole year.

01:15:58.428 --> 01:16:03.058
[Ami] So in twenty... in 2018,

01:16:03.100 --> 01:16:08.063
[Ami] on January 1st, I started posting daily on Weibo, one post every day.

01:16:08.063 --> 01:16:10.941
[Ami] I was so obsessed with it back then.

01:16:10.941 --> 01:16:16.029
[Ami] One night, I was at a KTV singing with my classmate, and he was scrolling through Weibo.

01:16:16.154 --> 01:16:17.447
[Ray] And you were posting about your planner?

01:16:17.614 --> 01:16:22.411
[Ami] I had already scheduled it to post. He refreshed his feed and said, "Hey,

01:16:22.411 --> 01:16:26.373
[Ami] how did you post an update today?" and meanwhile, I was singing.

01:16:26.373 --> 01:16:31.295
[Ami] He was so annoyed. I said, "Oh, I scheduled it this morning."

01:16:31.295 --> 01:16:34.673
[Ami] I would post one entry from my planner every day,

01:16:34.673 --> 01:16:40.804
[Ami] including some of my "small blessings" of the day—that was a popular concept at the time—

01:16:40.804 --> 01:16:45.767
[Ami] like if some small thing happened that day that I felt was worth celebrating.

01:16:45.767 --> 01:16:48.770
[Ami] I would pair that thought with a photo of my planner entry.

01:16:48.770 --> 01:16:53.692
[Ami] So I think that year was when I gained my first wave of social media followers.

01:16:53.692 --> 01:16:58.447
[Ami] It was a huge reward for me after completing that year-long challenge.

01:16:58.447 --> 01:17:04.328
[Ami] Then, you have to realize, no platform stays booming forever.

01:17:04.328 --> 01:17:10.167
[Ami] So after I graduated in 2018, OK, I bought a camera.

01:17:10.167 --> 01:17:13.545
[Ami] A Sony camera. Sponsor plug here.

01:17:13.545 --> 01:17:19.676
[Ami] Back then, having that Sony camera wasn't like now, when in China, maybe everyone

01:17:19.676 --> 01:17:24.681
[Ami] has a camera. Back then, if you had one of those Sony cameras,

01:17:24.681 --> 01:17:28.185
[Ami] you could shoot extremely beautiful and high-quality videos.

01:17:28.268 --> 01:17:34.024
[Ami] I had never tried making videos before; I'd mostly taken photos. So in 2018,

01:17:34.024 --> 01:17:36.193
[Ami] I thought, OK, Bilibili is now a big platform.

01:17:36.193 --> 01:17:40.280
[Ami] It signaled that we were entering the video era.

01:17:40.322 --> 01:17:45.827
[Ami] So I thought, I can't just take photos anymore, and I started shooting videos.

01:17:45.827 --> 01:17:50.874
[Ami] Then in 2018, I started making lifestyle content.

01:17:50.874 --> 01:17:54.127
[Ami] Because the audience for journaling content is relatively small,

01:17:54.127 --> 01:17:59.633
[Ami] but when you expand into lifestyle content, the platform might notice, "Oh, you shoot really well,"

01:17:59.633 --> 01:18:01.635
[Ami] and then it started promoting my videos.

01:18:01.635 --> 01:18:06.807
[Ami] So at that time, I had external support from the platform, and I also love creating content.

01:18:06.807 --> 01:18:11.520
[Ami] Plus, in that half-year period between graduation and starting my job,

01:18:11.520 --> 01:18:17.651
[Ami] I was looking for a job for a while, so I started creating video content then.

01:18:17.651 --> 01:18:22.531
[Ami] I kept doing it and I'm still doing it now. But later on,

01:18:22.531 --> 01:18:27.369
[Ami] about two years ago, short videos became popular, and people didn't want to watch long ones anymore.

01:18:27.369 --> 01:18:31.456
[Ami] Things change so fast. For example, platforms like Xiaohongshu in China,

01:18:31.456 --> 01:18:35.836
[Ami] and if you look at YouTube, it launched Shorts, and Instagram has Reels.

01:18:35.836 --> 01:18:37.504
[Ami] They all started focusing on short-form videos.

01:18:37.546 --> 01:18:42.300
[Ray] OK, so I guess Bilibili reached a bottleneck at that time. First,

01:18:42.300 --> 01:18:45.846
[Ray] you couldn't really monetize it.

01:18:45.846 --> 01:18:48.098
[Ray] Did you hit a bottleneck, or did the platform?

01:18:48.098 --> 01:18:49.808
[Ray] Both.

01:18:50.100 --> 01:18:53.770
[Ami] Right. Ad... advertisers stopped investing in the platform.

01:18:53.770 --> 01:18:59.526
[Ami] And I found there was no point posting there anymore, because making long-form videos requires a huge investment,

01:18:59.526 --> 01:19:03.655
[Ami] but when the return on that investment isn't very high,

01:19:03.655 --> 01:19:06.992
[Ami] you start looking for new avenues.

01:19:06.992 --> 01:19:12.914
[Ami] So, OK, I switched from long-form to short-form videos. And over the past two years, for example, on Xiaohongshu,

01:19:12.914 --> 01:19:17.669
[Ami] yes, mainly on Xiaohongshu, I've been more active and successful.

01:19:17.669 --> 01:19:22.632
[Ami] I found its commercial value far exceeded that of the previous platform.

01:19:22.632 --> 01:19:26.636
[Ami] So I felt that, OK, this change, this transition,

01:19:26.636 --> 01:19:32.350
[Ami] or rather, the decision to switch platforms, was the right one. So that's roughly my social media journey,

01:19:32.350 --> 01:19:35.770
[Ami] from photos to long-form videos, and then to short-form videos.

01:19:35.812 --> 01:19:41.026
[Ray] OK, before we talk about Xiaohongshu, there's something else I'd like to know more about.

01:19:41.026 --> 01:19:45.989
[Ray] I wanted to ask about that 365-day challenge on Weibo.

01:19:45.989 --> 01:19:51.703
[Ray] We often talk about getting positive feedback when doing things. For example, when you first started selling things, you'd see the sales numbers ticking up.

01:19:51.703 --> 01:19:55.832
[Ray] That provides great positive feedback. Was there any positive feedback in what you were doing?

01:19:55.832 --> 01:19:57.918
[Ray] What motivated you to stick with it?

01:19:57.918 --> 01:20:02.130
[Ray] Because I often hear people say, "I'm going to run this Twitter account,"

01:20:02.130 --> 01:20:07.010
[Ray] "and I'm going to post, just like you did, for 365 days. Every single day."

01:20:07.010 --> 01:20:12.057
[Ray] Or, "I'm going to do YouTube. I'll start by committing to a year, posting daily or whatever," right?

01:20:12.057 --> 01:20:16.186
[Ray] Many people make these vows, but most of them can't stick with it.

01:20:16.186 --> 01:20:19.022
[Ray] But why were you able to stick with it?

01:20:19.147 --> 01:20:22.776
[Ami] Back then, at the very beginning, I think it wasn't like it is now.

01:20:22.776 --> 01:20:27.864
[Ami] Now, I might feel a bit of pressure when I post something, worrying if anyone will see it or what they'll think.

01:20:27.864 --> 01:20:31.284
[Ami] But back then, I was just like, okay, I am who I am.

01:20:31.284 --> 01:20:35.539
[Ami] I'm actually quite confident in my own aesthetic taste.

01:20:35.539 --> 01:20:37.582
[Ami] I just felt that my work was better than others'.

01:20:37.582 --> 01:20:39.167
[Ami] I thought, why shouldn't I show it off?

01:20:39.167 --> 01:20:43.838
[Ami] So I thought, okay, when I first started posting, I honestly didn't care if people liked it

01:20:43.838 --> 01:20:49.052
[Ami] or commented. I just posted, I did my thing, and I didn't worry about

01:20:49.052 --> 01:20:54.850
[Ami] what the outcome would be. I wasn't goal-oriented, thinking I had to reach a certain target.

01:20:54.933 --> 01:21:00.814
[Ami] It was simple. I just wanted to post for 365 days. And also, I can be a bit...

01:21:00.814 --> 01:21:04.109
[Ami] a little obsessive, like I have a bit of OCD.

01:21:04.109 --> 01:21:09.864
[Ami] Because the posts I made at the time were titled 1-365, then 2-365.

01:21:09.864 --> 01:21:12.993
[Ami] If I saw that number sequence break, it would really bother me.

01:21:12.993 --> 01:21:17.622
[Ami] So that was probably one small thing that helped me stick with it.

01:21:17.622 --> 01:21:22.168
[Ami] Secondly, as you post more, you start getting some comments.

01:21:22.168 --> 01:21:25.714
[Ami] And it doesn't matter how small... even if you only get one comment,

01:21:25.714 --> 01:21:28.425
[Ami] you'll feel like, okay, someone likes it.

01:21:28.425 --> 01:21:31.136
[Ami] I think that's a huge encouragement.

01:21:31.177 --> 01:21:37.017
[Ami] For example, even now, I receive a lot of DMs. Sometimes, when I feel like I can't go on,

01:21:37.017 --> 01:21:42.564
[Ami] I've screenshotted their messages. It makes me feel like, okay, what I'm doing is meaningful.

01:21:42.564 --> 01:21:47.819
[Ami] Whether it's for others or for myself, this is something interesting.

01:21:47.819 --> 01:21:53.283
[Ami] Just like I said before, I quit my previous company because I felt it wasn't interesting.

01:21:53.283 --> 01:21:56.912
[Ami] So if this is interesting, I'll keep on doing it.

01:21:57.037 --> 01:21:58.913
[Ray] Mmm, so is doing Xiaohongshu interesting?

01:21:58.913 --> 01:22:02.792
[Ray] I see you're very active on Xiaohongshu. One day you're in Japan,

01:22:02.792 --> 01:22:04.920
[Ray] the next day you're in Japan again.

01:22:04.961 --> 01:22:05.211
[Ami] (chuckles)

01:22:05.253 --> 01:22:10.884
[Ami] I think the "interesting" part of it is a bit different from simply creating content.

01:22:10.884 --> 01:22:14.471
[Ami] It's a combination of being interesting and having a sense of achievement.

01:22:14.471 --> 01:22:19.225
[Ami] For example, I never would have thought that when I bought my first Sony camera,

01:22:19.225 --> 01:22:21.019
[Ami] I never would have thought... okay, you know?

01:22:21.061 --> 01:22:25.357
[Ami] The camera you're holding now would one day sponsor you to create content.

01:22:25.357 --> 01:22:30.737
[Ami] For me, that's something that gives me a great sense of achievement.

01:22:30.737 --> 01:22:35.575
[Ami] So whether I'm creating content or collaborating with brands,

01:22:35.575 --> 01:22:39.871
[Ami] I always feel that it's exhausting but joyful.

01:22:39.871 --> 01:22:45.043
[Ami] And at the same time, of course, like I said, it's my ikigai. It's what I love,

01:22:45.043 --> 01:22:50.048
[Ami] what I'm good at, and it can also help me earn money. That combination is very, very rare.

01:22:50.048 --> 01:22:54.469
[Ami] So for me, it's not just that I'm very interested in doing this,

01:22:54.469 --> 01:23:00.308
[Ami] but it also brings a sense of achievement, and on top of that, it helps me earn money. So I think, why wouldn't I do it,

01:23:00.308 --> 01:23:00.684
[Ami] right?

01:23:00.725 --> 01:23:01.935
[Ray] So have you made money?

01:23:02.018 --> 01:23:04.187
[Ami] Mmm, quite a bit.

01:23:04.187 --> 01:23:05.188
[Ami] (laughs)

01:23:05.271 --> 01:23:12.070
[Ray] So, in your total entrepreneurial income, we just mentioned a net profit figure of 70 for this year.

01:23:12.070 --> 01:23:15.782
[Ray] How much of that comes from being a content creator?

01:23:16.199 --> 01:23:21.204
[Ami] In terms of percentage, I'd say content creation is about 60%,

01:23:21.204 --> 01:23:23.248
[Ami] and my stationery brand is about 40%.

01:23:23.581 --> 01:23:24.249
[Ray] Mmm.

01:23:24.290 --> 01:23:25.875
[Ami] But for the content creation part,

01:23:25.875 --> 01:23:30.797
[Ami] I think it purely depends on whether I want to accept that many brand deals.

01:23:30.839 --> 01:23:32.465
[Ray] Which means there's still a lot of potential.

01:23:32.674 --> 01:23:33.717
[Ami] Uh, yeah.

01:23:34.217 --> 01:23:34.426
[Ami] (laughs)

01:23:34.509 --> 01:23:35.510
[Ray] (laughs)

01:23:35.510 --> 01:23:37.220
[Ami] But well, that depends on whether brands reach out.

01:23:37.303 --> 01:23:40.640
[Ray] Brands, or rather, brands that you actually like.

01:23:40.724 --> 01:23:44.853
[Ami] Right. Because I feel... mmm, when we talk about side hustles,

01:23:44.853 --> 01:23:50.775
[Ami] I think it's best not to have just one. I think you should have at least two.

01:23:50.775 --> 01:23:56.281
[Ami] I think it's good to have a plan B. For instance, with content creation,

01:23:56.281 --> 01:24:00.118
[Ami] it's easy... I'm actually trying to find the right balance now.

01:24:00.118 --> 01:24:03.872
[Ami] Sometimes, you might slowly turn into just an advertising account.

01:24:03.872 --> 01:24:10.045
[Ami] That's a state I really don't want to be in, where people see you and think, "Oh,

01:24:10.045 --> 01:24:13.089
[Ami] another video, another ad." I don't want that.

01:24:13.089 --> 01:24:16.718
[Ami] But if I didn't have the income from my stationery brand,

01:24:16.718 --> 01:24:19.637
[Ami] I could easily become an advertising account like that.

01:24:19.637 --> 01:24:24.309
[Ami] So for me, having these two income streams

01:24:24.309 --> 01:24:28.646
[Ami] allows me to maintain a state where I can do what makes me happy

01:24:28.646 --> 01:24:33.234
[Ami] and also earn money, without giving my audience a bad impression.

01:24:33.234 --> 01:24:37.280
[Ami] Like you mentioned about the content I post on this platform. So I think if you're going to have a side hustle, master one first,

01:24:37.280 --> 01:24:39.491
[Ami] and then figure out when to start the second one.

01:24:39.616 --> 01:24:41.826
[Ray] I think that's a great point. Always have a plan B.

01:24:41.826 --> 01:24:45.663
[Ray] I think that's also a very important way to maintain your own integrity.

01:24:45.663 --> 01:24:47.707
[Ray] It's like for us as finance content creators.

01:24:47.707 --> 01:24:53.630
[Ray] If we only relied on our sponsors for money, then I'd probably...

01:24:53.630 --> 01:24:57.509
[Ray] I'd just be working for them. I'd become their salesperson. That's why having my own products,

01:24:57.509 --> 01:25:01.930
[Ray] or another business, allows me to maintain that integrity and do what makes me happy.

01:25:01.930 --> 01:25:04.724
[Ray] Speaking of happiness, let me quickly ask you two questions.

01:25:04.724 --> 01:25:10.230
[Ray] First, uh, in your journey as a content creator, have you ever been unhappy?

01:25:10.230 --> 01:25:14.317
[Ray] Of course, you don't have to mention the specific sponsor's name.

01:25:14.317 --> 01:25:17.070
[Ray] And second, have you ever felt burned out?

01:25:17.153 --> 01:25:17.529
[Ami] Mmm...

01:25:17.570 --> 01:25:22.408
[Ami] As for being unhappy, I try my best not to do things that make me unhappy.

01:25:22.408 --> 01:25:26.329
[Ami] I'm quite strict when it comes to selecting brands to work with.

01:25:26.329 --> 01:25:32.085
[Ami] Because sometimes, what I want most is to create an interesting video,

01:25:32.085 --> 01:25:37.173
[Ami] rather than just being a mouthpiece to promote your product.

01:25:37.173 --> 01:25:42.428
[Ami] So, if we're talking about unhappy moments, I can only say I feel that way...

01:25:42.428 --> 01:25:45.557
[Ami] ...when I feel very burned out. That's when I feel unhappy.

01:25:45.557 --> 01:25:51.229
[Ami] But I think that completely depends on how you...

01:25:51.271 --> 01:25:54.816
[Ami] manage the pressure, whether you want to take on so much. Do you want to earn more money,

01:25:54.816 --> 01:26:00.697
[Ami] or do you want to strike a good balance, where you're earning money but not getting too exhausted?

01:26:00.697 --> 01:26:03.408
[Ami] I think that's something you have to manage for yourself.

01:26:03.408 --> 01:26:09.038
[Ami] So far, I think it's been okay. I try my best. If I really get burned out,

01:26:09.163 --> 01:26:11.499
[Ami] it means I was just being too greedy, you know?

01:26:11.499 --> 01:26:13.918
[Ami] Like, if I really wanted to,

01:26:13.918 --> 01:26:18.464
[Ami] I could take on a lot of ads. I'd make a lot of money, but I'd burn out.

01:26:18.464 --> 01:26:19.841
[Ami] And then I'd be unhappy.

01:26:19.841 --> 01:26:22.051
[Ami] So I'd rather tell myself to earn a bit less,

01:26:22.051 --> 01:26:28.099
[Ami] and maintain a relatively stable state, both mentally and physically.

01:26:28.099 --> 01:26:29.434
[Ami] I think that's better.

01:26:29.475 --> 01:26:34.689
[Ami] So overall, I think I've had relatively few unhappy moments so far.

01:26:34.689 --> 01:26:38.860
[Ami] Or rather, I'm actively trying to manage it so I don't reach a state of burnout.

01:26:38.860 --> 01:26:39.360
[Ray] Mmm.

01:26:39.402 --> 01:26:45.074
[Ray] We mentioned earlier that side hustles are just one of many ways to make money.

01:26:45.074 --> 01:26:46.576
[Ray] So, what's your next plan?

01:26:46.576 --> 01:26:48.077
[Ray] What are you going to do next?

01:26:48.328 --> 01:26:54.167
[Ami] I think my next plan... honestly, even though the income figure we talked about,

01:26:54.167 --> 01:27:00.215
[Ami] might not seem like a lot to your audience, for me it's already at a point

01:27:00.215 --> 01:27:02.675
[Ami] where I feel it's enough.

01:27:02.675 --> 01:27:07.680
[Ami] Of course, I'm still motivated to earn more. Everyone wants that.

01:27:07.722 --> 01:27:09.933
[Ray] So you haven't experienced lifestyle inflation?

01:27:09.933 --> 01:27:11.434
[Ray] Like buying a bigger house or a bigger car?

01:27:11.434 --> 01:27:12.185
[Ami] Oh...

01:27:12.268 --> 01:27:16.814
[Ami] I've had those thoughts, but then I thought, okay, the amount I'm earning now...

01:27:16.814 --> 01:27:20.109
[Ami] ...is far from enough to buy a house in Shanghai, you know.

01:27:20.109 --> 01:27:21.986
[Ami] But I still wondered, hey, maybe I could.

01:27:22.028 --> 01:27:27.408
[Ami] I even thought about buying a small house in Japan. But after considering everything, I realized,

01:27:27.450 --> 01:27:30.119
[Ami] if I were to buy one, it would create a lot of...

01:27:30.119 --> 01:27:35.875
[Ami] worries, or rather, it would become a huge burden. So why would I make that decision?

01:27:35.875 --> 01:27:41.881
[Ami] But then I thought... actually, last night I had a very scary dream.

01:27:41.881 --> 01:27:46.552
[Ami] I dreamed it was the end of the world. But I remember my whole family was huddled together,

01:27:46.552 --> 01:27:49.555
[Ami] and in that moment, I didn't feel any regrets.

01:27:49.639 --> 01:27:49.764
[Ray] Mmm.

01:27:49.806 --> 01:27:55.853
[Ami] And it made me think, "Oh, my current state in life is that I don't want to..."

01:27:55.853 --> 01:28:01.317
[Ami] ...I don't want to greedily pursue more things. I think maintaining what I have now is good enough.

01:28:01.401 --> 01:28:07.740
[Ami] It's like, if tomorrow were the end of the world, but today there's nothing else you want to do,

01:28:07.740 --> 01:28:13.329
[Ami] or anything you'd regret not doing, then I think maintaining the status quo is perfectly fine.

01:28:13.329 --> 01:28:16.541
[Ami] So... but of course, I definitely want to earn more.

01:28:16.541 --> 01:28:21.713
[Ami] But if it means I have to exhaust myself to earn more money,

01:28:21.713 --> 01:28:23.256
[Ami] I probably won't do that.

01:28:23.297 --> 01:28:28.636
[Ray] So your driving force isn't more desire. Then what is... could you summarize it?

01:28:28.636 --> 01:28:30.221
[Ray] What is your driving force?

01:28:30.221 --> 01:28:31.305
[Ray] The driving force to earn more money.

01:28:31.806 --> 01:28:36.436
[Ami] My driving force for earning more money is that it makes me feel valuable.

01:28:36.602 --> 01:28:37.145
[Ray] Mmm.

01:28:37.186 --> 01:28:42.275
[Ami] I feel that... Of course, money is a standard of measurement.

01:28:42.275 --> 01:28:46.362
[Ami] If you can earn money from something, it means it's meaningful, or valuable.

01:28:46.362 --> 01:28:52.160
[Ami] That indirectly boosts my sense of personal value.

01:28:52.160 --> 01:28:57.582
[Ami] For example, if I create something and it sells well, that means my design is good, and then I feel, "Oh,

01:28:57.582 --> 01:29:01.336
[Ami] I'm a very capable or valuable person."

01:29:01.419 --> 01:29:06.341
[Ami] And of course, that sense of achievement is perhaps more external. But I think

01:29:06.341 --> 01:29:11.846
[Ami] if I can take this story of making money, or, how should I put it,

01:29:11.846 --> 01:29:16.309
[Ami] my experience... because to others, I might seem like a kind of successful person.

01:29:16.309 --> 01:29:21.272
[Ami] If I can share that story, that would be another kind of fulfillment.

01:29:21.439 --> 01:29:25.860
[Ami] But first, I have to achieve what's considered success by worldly standards.

01:29:25.860 --> 01:29:29.614
[Ami] Only then can I share this experience of success with more people.

01:29:29.655 --> 01:29:32.241
[Ray] So your next plan is to share your experiences?

01:29:32.283 --> 01:29:38.372
[Ami] Uh, I hope to reach an even higher level of so-called success first, and then I can share it with more people.

01:29:38.372 --> 01:29:41.626
[Ami] Or rather, so that people will feel my success is genuinely impressive.

01:29:41.626 --> 01:29:47.381
[Ray] Mmm, you also have a YouTube channel. We've actually collaborated before. Uh...

01:29:47.381 --> 01:29:49.675
[Ray] but it seems you haven't been focusing on YouTube since then.

01:29:49.675 --> 01:29:53.387
[Ray] Do you plan to start posting on it again in the future?

01:29:53.596 --> 01:29:59.227
[Ami] Uh, of course. My thinking is, right now, for example, with Xiaohongshu,

01:29:59.227 --> 01:30:03.981
[Ami] the commercial value is quite high. But I actually want to create more content

01:30:03.981 --> 01:30:06.943
[Ami] about my personal growth.

01:30:06.943 --> 01:30:10.863
[Ami] In my overall content plan for social media,

01:30:10.863 --> 01:30:15.743
[Ami] the "productivity tech" part is more suitable for sponsorships.

01:30:15.743 --> 01:30:21.374
[Ami] But what I really want to share more is my personal growth, including conversations like the one we're having today. Or, I have

01:30:21.374 --> 01:30:26.921
[Ami] a strong desire to express myself. But right now, making money is the priority.

01:30:26.921 --> 01:30:28.548
[Ami] But for my next plan in the future,

01:30:28.548 --> 01:30:33.678
[Ami] I'd probably focus more on sharing my life story with everyone.

01:30:33.719 --> 01:30:39.684
[Ray] Okay, everyone, look at the screen. We'll put her YouTube, uh, handle up.

01:30:39.684 --> 01:30:43.146
[Ray] We'll put her channel link up. So after this episode airs,

01:30:43.146 --> 01:30:49.193
[Ray] I hope we can see Ami sharing more of her life experiences

01:30:49.193 --> 01:30:50.069
[Ray] and journey on her YouTube channel.

01:30:50.111 --> 01:30:54.282
[Ray] I must be very frank. When Ami shares these things with us,

01:30:54.282 --> 01:30:57.702
[Ray] many people might think that as a content creator,

01:30:57.702 --> 01:31:02.331
[Ray] it seems like everything you talk about is money, money, money. You know, business.

01:31:02.331 --> 01:31:05.835
[Ray] And how my content is linked to commercial opportunities.

01:31:05.835 --> 01:31:08.880
[Ray] But in reality, that's the nature of the business.

01:31:08.880 --> 01:31:13.968
[Ray] But what's really commendable about Ami is that she's so sincere in talking about it.

01:31:13.968 --> 01:31:17.138
[Ray] She isn't shy about saying, "This is my plan, this is to make money."

01:31:17.138 --> 01:31:21.601
[Ray] But at the same time, it's also, as you said, your ikigai. It's something you want to do.

01:31:21.601 --> 01:31:26.856
[Ray] You don't let them conflict. And at the same time, you have many other pursuits.

01:31:26.856 --> 01:31:28.191
[Ray] And you pursue those as well.

01:31:28.191 --> 01:31:31.444
[Ray] So I think being able to speak about it so frankly is actually better.

01:31:31.444 --> 01:31:34.197
[Ray] I think we should really appreciate that.

01:31:34.197 --> 01:31:36.032
[Ray] So finally, I want to ask Ami,

01:31:36.115 --> 01:31:38.743
[Ray] I know that right now in China, uh, there are many young people

01:31:38.784 --> 01:31:42.997
[Ray] who are somehow a bit lost, especially with the current economic situation.

01:31:42.997 --> 01:31:47.627
[Ray] As we're recording this, there are all sorts of tariffs and big sticks being wielded, right?

01:31:47.627 --> 01:31:49.712
[Ray] So a lot of people feel very lost.

01:31:49.754 --> 01:31:53.925
[Ray] A couple of days ago, I made a video about entrepreneurship, and someone left a comment on Twitter.

01:31:55.134 --> 01:31:58.971
[Ray] They said, "I'm a student, I can't find my direction, what should I do?"

01:31:59.013 --> 01:32:03.309
[Ray] Or many people say, "I also want a side hustle, I want to start a so-called business,"

01:32:03.309 --> 01:32:06.270
[Ray] "but how do I even start?"

01:32:06.270 --> 01:32:08.147
[Ray] "I don't know what I should do."

01:32:08.147 --> 01:32:11.984
[Ray] So for these people, your peers,

01:32:11.984 --> 01:32:14.779
[Ray] what advice would you have for them?

01:32:14.779 --> 01:32:17.323
[Ray] Let's get a sneak peek of your future video content.

01:32:17.490 --> 01:32:21.160
[Ami] I think, throughout my own personal development,

01:32:21.160 --> 01:32:26.123
[Ami] a piece of advice that might be universally applicable for everyone is,

01:32:26.123 --> 01:32:31.712
[Ami] first, have a hobby. Because I feel, especially for... I don't know, maybe for kids in East Asia,

01:32:31.712 --> 01:32:33.297
[Ami] It's like when I was a kid,

01:32:33.297 --> 01:32:37.969
[Ami] everything I learned growing up was very utilitarian.

01:32:37.969 --> 01:32:43.307
[Ami] For example, when I learned piano as a child, I actually kind of liked it at first, but later on,

01:32:43.307 --> 01:32:49.146
[Ami] you know, because of things like proficiency exams, which could get you bonus points on the college entrance exam or something similar,

01:32:49.146 --> 01:32:54.277
[Ami] it became very utilitarian. And then when that hobby eventually becomes something you

01:32:54.277 --> 01:32:58.489
[Ami] have to do, I think it changes for the worse.

01:32:58.489 --> 01:33:01.450
[Ami] But after we grow up, it's very rare...

01:33:01.450 --> 01:33:06.706
[Ami] very few people get the chance to explore what their hobbies are. For many people, for example, if I...

01:33:06.706 --> 01:33:11.043
[Ami] if I ask my friends what hobbies they have, they might not be able to come up with anything.

01:33:11.043 --> 01:33:16.048
[Ami] Outside of work and life, they don't have something that's their own,

01:33:16.048 --> 01:33:18.134
[Ami] a little area for personal development.

01:33:18.217 --> 01:33:21.679
[Ami] I think that's something now, if you have the time,

01:33:21.679 --> 01:33:27.643
[Ami] I believe you must find something like that for yourself. This includes my side businesses, my two side businesses,

01:33:27.643 --> 01:33:30.896
[Ami] whether it's making videos or creating cultural products,

01:33:30.896 --> 01:33:36.193
[Ami] I think they all stem from a genuine love I have deep down, for example, for video,

01:33:36.193 --> 01:33:40.823
[Ami] or a love for design and journaling. It's built on a foundation of genuine passion.

01:33:40.823 --> 01:33:46.621
[Ami] So I think if you have a hobby, it can help you find your own direction,

01:33:46.621 --> 01:33:51.792
[Ami] rather than being a little spark that's just following a direction someone else told you to take.

01:33:51.834 --> 01:33:57.340
[Ami] And secondly, I think it's crucial to keep learning, to be a lifelong learner.

01:33:57.340 --> 01:34:02.261
[Ami] Because for myself, since graduating, perhaps, uh,

01:34:02.261 --> 01:34:05.264
[Ami] during my working years, I truly, I... I...

01:34:05.264 --> 01:34:10.561
[Ami] I can totally relate to that feeling of just wanting to lie down after work. You know, you get home and you

01:34:10.561 --> 01:34:12.813
[Ami] just want to scroll through short videos.

01:34:12.813 --> 01:34:16.859
[Ami] But I think if you can squeeze out a little bit of time to learn,

01:34:16.859 --> 01:34:20.696
[Ami] or to look into some modern, cutting-edge things, I think it would be very helpful.

01:34:20.696 --> 01:34:24.367
[Ami] Take my own personal example. In our community,

01:34:24.367 --> 01:34:29.872
[Ami] we had that AI co-creation group before, right? The one Zhu Qing and the others organized. I was in that group,

01:34:29.872 --> 01:34:35.252
[Ami] and honestly, before that, when it came to AI, my view was basically just, okay, DeepSeek is what I use.

01:34:35.252 --> 01:34:38.881
[Ami] I'd ask it a question, and its answer was essentially no different from a search engine.

01:34:38.881 --> 01:34:41.550
[Ami] But after I joined that group,

01:34:41.634 --> 01:34:47.181
[Ami] okay, I learned a lot of things during the process of understanding AI.

01:34:47.223 --> 01:34:52.061
[Ami] OK, I discovered there's AI image generation software, and that software

01:34:52.061 --> 01:34:57.066
[Ami] has helped me with my creative product design. I've already started using it in my design process.

01:34:57.066 --> 01:35:02.988
[Ami] And also with things like my knowledge management. Before, when I read some books, I might read them and then not...

01:35:02.988 --> 01:35:08.869
[Ami] go back to review them. But now, with what we call an AI agent, it can help me review and organize the material.

01:35:08.869 --> 01:35:11.664
[Ami] All of this came about while I was learning about AI.

01:35:11.706 --> 01:35:17.712
[Ami] It was in the process that I discovered this, like a new little subplot, as we'd say,

01:35:17.712 --> 01:35:22.133
[Ami] or a new item. It's like in the game of life, you've picked up a new tool,

01:35:22.133 --> 01:35:24.468
[Ami] and that came through the whole learning process.

01:35:24.468 --> 01:35:30.099
[Ami] But if I myself hadn't gone and, okay, hadn't researched this thing, or if I didn't have the interest,

01:35:30.099 --> 01:35:32.226
[Ami] or if I didn't have the need to look into it,

01:35:32.226 --> 01:35:37.022
[Ami] then perhaps a tool like this would never have appeared on my path in life.

01:35:37.022 --> 01:35:42.403
[Ami] So I think for young people, the world today is, I truly feel, constantly changing.

01:35:42.403 --> 01:35:48.159
[Ami] A lot of things are changing, but what can remain constant is your motivation to keep learning,

01:35:48.159 --> 01:35:53.998
[Ami] or your state of being a learner. It's not like you finish your university studies and then you quit that kind of

01:35:53.998 --> 01:35:56.917
[Ami] educational system and stop learning new things.

01:35:56.959 --> 01:36:01.547
[Ami] This is something I think might be a helpful piece of advice for everyone,

01:36:01.547 --> 01:36:03.591
[Ami] a little tip, you could say.

01:36:03.799 --> 01:36:09.180
[Ray] Great. You just mentioned passion, "rè'ài." I almost mispronounced it. I think I might be a bit hungry.

01:36:09.221 --> 01:36:14.727
[Ray] OK, yes, you mentioned passion. You said earlier that your former boss,

01:36:14.727 --> 01:36:17.438
[Ray] that he had a vision, and you were the one doing the propaganda for him,

01:36:17.438 --> 01:36:21.025
[Ray] that brainwashing leader of yours. Elon Musk has said the same thing,

01:36:21.025 --> 01:36:24.487
[Ray] that you wake up in the morning with something you're excited to do.

01:36:24.487 --> 01:36:30.576
[Ray] To be honest, when I hear that, and I think many people feel this way, kids from East Asia like us actually feel a bit lost.

01:36:30.576 --> 01:36:35.706
[Ray] I really don't feel like I wake up in the morning with something I'm eager to do.

01:36:35.706 --> 01:36:39.668
[Ray] This... this phenomenon happened to me for a long time.

01:36:39.668 --> 01:36:43.297
[Ray] Because, in my earlier days, maybe we were a bit different,

01:36:43.297 --> 01:36:48.427
[Ray] for me it was more about the need to survive. Hey, I needed to make money.

01:36:48.427 --> 01:36:49.970
[Ray] The usual way of making money,

01:36:49.970 --> 01:36:54.809
[Ray] working a regular job to make money was, for me, simply too slow.

01:36:54.809 --> 01:36:56.352
[Ray] I needed to make money faster.

01:36:56.352 --> 01:37:00.314
[Ray] It wasn't driven by any kind of passion; it was driven by necessity.

01:37:00.314 --> 01:37:02.441
[Ray] And in the end, I survived.

01:37:02.441 --> 01:37:07.196
[Ray] But for a while, I felt a bit... a bit lost. I lost myself. What do I actually like?

01:37:07.196 --> 01:37:11.283
[Ray] I didn't really have hobbies. Like you mentioned, the "small-town test-taker." I wasn't even a good one,

01:37:11.283 --> 01:37:12.952
[Ray] so I didn't have any passion for that either.

01:37:12.952 --> 01:37:16.497
[Ray] We're actually quite similar. The reason this channel exists,

01:37:16.497 --> 01:37:22.169
[Ray] this podcast exists, is also out of my... my passion for photography, videography,

01:37:22.169 --> 01:37:24.421
[Ray] and for audio and things like that.

01:37:24.421 --> 01:37:27.132
[Ray] I've been listening to podcasts for over a decade.

01:37:27.132 --> 01:37:31.762
[Ray] I was probably among the first group of people in China to listen to English podcasts.

01:37:31.762 --> 01:37:34.682
[Ray] I started listening as soon as the podcast app came out.

01:37:34.682 --> 01:37:40.396
[Ray] So, a lot of the time, we need to search. It's not necessarily that you don't have it.

01:37:40.396 --> 01:37:45.192
[Ray] You need to truly rediscover, to really think about, "Oh, what is it that I'm passionate about?"

01:37:45.192 --> 01:37:46.610
[Ray] What is it?

01:37:46.610 --> 01:37:52.283
[Ray] If you really don't have anything, you should find a way—it's the underlying logic—to cultivate something you're passionate about.

01:37:52.283 --> 01:37:55.327
[Ray] You make yourself an interesting person, a person with passion.

01:37:55.369 --> 01:38:00.583
[Ray] So when I see you, I feel that you're full of energy. I think that's extremely important.

01:38:00.583 --> 01:38:03.127
[Ray] And, uh, continuous learning.

01:38:03.127 --> 01:38:09.049
[Ray] So, uh, you just mentioned AI. Hey, let me... let me see if I brought it... ah, look.

01:38:09.049 --> 01:38:14.138
[Ray] Do you still have the thing we made for our community back then, this... this...

01:38:14.138 --> 01:38:15.139
[Ray] this logo, you could say.

01:38:15.139 --> 01:38:19.852
[Ray] We actually used some AI for it. This actually reflects two points we've talked about today.

01:38:19.852 --> 01:38:23.647
[Ray] The first is to keep learning. Of course, there was this AI stuff,

01:38:23.647 --> 01:38:27.443
[Ray] and I said, "Ami, we should use AI to whip something up. Won't that be faster?"

01:38:27.443 --> 01:38:27.902
[Ray] Right?

01:38:27.943 --> 01:38:28.736
[Ray] It'd be quicker.

01:38:28.819 --> 01:38:31.405
[Ray] So we went ahead and did it.

01:38:31.405 --> 01:38:38.037
[Ray] And the second thing reflects a point you just made, which is, uh, "done is better than perfect."

01:38:38.037 --> 01:38:42.041
[Ray] Right, we just made it. We made it very quickly, including the things we designed.

01:38:42.041 --> 01:38:42.625
[Ray] We got it done quickly.

01:38:42.666 --> 01:38:48.631
[Ray] I also got to experience it with you, uh, taking a product from zero to one, from nothing to something.

01:38:48.631 --> 01:38:51.342
[Ray] Because I had never made any physical things before.

01:38:51.342 --> 01:38:56.555
[Ray] Then we made that notebook, and when we finally produced it, I also felt that sense of excitement.

01:38:56.597 --> 01:39:02.603
[Ray] So after completing that cycle, I realized, oh, I still want to do what I love, to create, uh,

01:39:02.603 --> 01:39:05.648
[Ray] products that satisfy my needs.

01:39:05.689 --> 01:39:12.029
[Ray] Uh, I feel like I have some answers now. And I look forward to collaborating with you in the future, Ami,

01:39:12.029 --> 01:39:16.533
[Ray] to co-create some interesting products and attract people like us.

01:39:16.575 --> 01:39:22.039
[Ray] OK, thank you, Ami. Everyone, please be sure to follow her channel. We'll talk again next time.

01:39:22.206 --> 01:39:22.915
[Ami] OK, thank you.

01:39:22.957 --> 01:39:28.712
[Ray] Uh, I'm really looking forward to it. In a year's time, hey, let's see what kind of changes you've undergone.

01:39:28.921 --> 01:39:29.213
[Ami] OK.

01:39:29.254 --> 01:39:30.923
[Ray] OK.

01:39:30.965 --> 01:39:34.218
[Ray] OK, that's a wrap. That's a wrap. This was great.

